{"summary":"Rebuilt the Story proposal as a grounded closed-loop tragedy. The protagonist never sees another version of himself; environmental details, ordinary gameplay choices and persistent block states reveal that he built the portal after losing Moss. The remaining blockers are version-specific fire, lava-ignition, wolf-damage and creeper tests, plus human approval of the tragic Hardcore ending.","document":"# Story plan — The Portal That Nobody Made\n\n> This is an unlocked Story proposal. It develops the showrunner note without recording a creative lock, audience result or production validation.\n\n## Development status\n\nThis revision replaces the rescue-choice version with a closed-loop tragedy. The protagonist never sees another version of himself. He first encounters the effects of his own future actions as environmental contradictions, enters the portal, builds a life with a tamed wolf in the same ravine at an earlier time, loses the wolf in a fire, and constructs the portal in a desperate attempt to go back again. A creeper kills him during an improvised lava ignition; the blast changes the lava-and-wood geometry, allowing ordinary fire spread to activate the frame. That is the portal his earlier personal self found.\n\nThe wolf’s working name is **Moss**. Moss has no pen or doghouse. The relationship is established through normal play: taming, travelling, gathering materials, feeding, building around an inquisitive companion, and using the sit command only when the protagonist enters the dangerous ravine mine.\n\nThe proposed world is Hardcore so the final death is terminal. The mode, exact Minecraft edition and mechanical chain remain unlocked until human approval and reproducibility testing.\n\n## Audience promise\n\nA lone player exploring an unvisited part of his private world finds a burned cabin and a lit portal that cannot belong there. The portal takes him not to the Nether but to the same untouched ravine several days earlier. As he survives, builds and bonds with a wolf, practical construction decisions begin producing the shapes he remembers from the ruin. The intended pleasure is recognising that every strange block, missing support and blast scar came from a normal action whose meaning was initially hidden.\n\nThe central questions are:\n\n- Who built and lit the portal?\n- Why does the new homestead become the ruin seen before it existed?\n- Can foreknowledge prevent the fire and save Moss?\n\nThe story would break its promise if another player or distant silhouette supplied the mystery, if an invisible force corrected history, if Moss were kept in an invented enclosure, if the protagonist delayed rescue to save replaceable blocks, or if the portal lit merely because an explosion was treated as magical fire.\n\n## Dramatic opening and relationship grounding\n\n### Cold open\n\nThe film opens inside the later fire. The protagonist ignores a chest and burning wall, empties his water bucket towards the room where Moss is sitting, and breaks directly towards the wolf. Moss is visible through flame for a moment. The protagonist reaches forward; the image cuts before the outcome.\n\nA restrained narration line may frame the promise without lying: **‘By the time I understood the portal, I was already late.’**\n\nThis fragment lasts roughly thirty-five seconds. It establishes immediate danger and makes clear that the protagonist is not choosing the house over the wolf.\n\n### Return to the beginning\n\nThe film then moves to the protagonist’s earlier personal experience. During a solo expedition far from his established base, he reaches a ravine whose coordinates and map edge show that he has not visited it before. On the west rim stands the cobblestone remainder of a burned cabin. Its wooden upper structure is mostly absent. A stair descends towards a north-wall cave, where an active obsidian frame stands beside a fresh blast cavity, exposed lava and the remains of a plank ignition cradle.\n\nThere is no body, dropped collar, sign, book or glimpse of another avatar. The evidence is construction and damage. The protagonist inspects the blocks, confirms that the portal is real, and chooses to enter.\n\n### Building the bond\n\nThe portal ejects him into the same cave several days earlier. The frame, cabin and player-placed stair do not exist. With night approaching and no return portal, he makes a temporary shelter on the only level shelf above the ravine.\n\nA skeleton encounter supplies bones through ordinary gameplay. He uses them to tame a nearby wolf and calls it Moss. The shelter then grows into a compact spruce-and-cobblestone cabin through a short series of lived scenes rather than a decorative montage alone:\n\n- Moss follows while the protagonist gathers spruce and repeatedly occupies the next block position during construction.\n- The protagonist feeds Moss after ordinary damage and waits for the tail to rise before continuing.\n- They return together at dusk; the protagonist closes the door, places food in the furnace and sits Moss beside the bed only before descending towards lava.\n- On returning from the mine, he releases Moss immediately, and the wolf follows him through the next round of chores.\n- A repeated wide shot records the cabin, the ravine stair and the two of them crossing the west ledge together.\n\nThese scenes give the later sit command emotional and mechanical meaning. It is a familiar safety routine, not confinement.\n\n## Story in world chronology\n\n1. The protagonist’s pre-travel self is several days away on a solo expedition. His later travelling self is ejected into the untouched ravine through a temporary portal plane. The two versions remain geographically separate and never see one another.\n2. The travelling protagonist camps on the west shelf, tames Moss with locally obtained bones and gradually builds the spruce-and-cobblestone cabin, stone chest recess and ravine stair later seen as ruins.\n3. Terrain dictates several distinctive decisions: the foundation bends around a granite seam, the stair anchors to a narrow stone rib, and one stripped-spruce support passes behind a stone-clad section above the lava shelf.\n4. The protagonist recognises those shapes from the ruin he saw before entering the portal. He decides to leave with Moss at dawn. Before leaving, he tries to neutralise the lava beneath the stair and collect enough obsidian for a controlled return frame.\n5. Moss is seated beside the bed because the lava shelf is dangerous. The protagonist pours water over the visible lava and mines the cooled blocks. Removing the final accessible block opens a concealed lateral lava flow. Water cools the face he can see, but the side flow reaches the hidden spruce support behind the stone cladding.\n6. Fire travels through that continuous support and into separated parts of the cabin. The protagonist immediately abandons the structure, routes his single water source towards Moss and breaks through the shortest wall. He reaches the sitting place one damage interval too late. Moss dies in the fire; no collar or invented keepsake drops.\n7. Following the missing-block path back down reveals that the fire, the surviving stone shell and the opening ruin are the same event. Grief converts the portal from a mystery into a proposed second chance.\n8. Using the obsidian he mined, the protagonist builds the exact economical frame in the north-wall cave. The water-cooled cobblestone foot, asymmetry and remaining plank position reproduce the details from the opening.\n9. His flint and steel and spare iron were lost with the cabin. Rather than pause to mine and smelt, he attempts an ordinary emergency ignition: lava outside the frame, with spruce positioned so a fire block may form inside it. Water used during the rescue has washed a torch from a lateral branch, leaving an approach dark.\n10. A creeper wanders from that branch while the protagonist waits for the ignition. At close range it detonates and kills the already damaged protagonist. The explosion does not directly create fire. It breaks the cobblestone lip retaining the placed lava and removes part of the cradle. Lava reaches the remaining spruce; a subsequent fire spread places flame inside the valid frame and activates it.\n11. The protagonist’s drops fall into lava or fire. The portal remains active beside the blast cavity. Later in world chronology, his earlier personal self reaches the ravine, finds the burned cabin and enters that same portal, beginning the journey just described.\n\n## Personal journey and audience presentation\n\nThe film uses a cold-open fragment, then follows the protagonist’s personal order:\n\n1. **Fire without context:** a brief image of the attempted rescue ends before Moss’s fate is known.\n2. **The ruin before the home:** the protagonist explores the burned cabin and reads its surviving construction without knowing it is his work.\n3. **The impossible threshold:** the active portal, blast cavity, cobblestone foot, exposed lava and incomplete ignition cradle are inspected. He enters by choice.\n4. **The ravine before construction:** the same geography is pristine and the portal frame is absent. Persistent natural landmarks establish an earlier state rather than another world.\n5. **A life made in the gap:** survival play, taming and building establish Moss and the cabin as lived experience.\n6. **Recognition through design:** the granite notch, foundation bend, chest recess and stair rib reproduce the remembered ruin. The protagonist accepts that he saw the future of this home.\n7. **A practical prevention plan:** he chooses departure with Moss, stone-clads the stair and attempts to remove the lava hazard before dawn.\n8. **Prevention becomes cause:** obsidian mining releases the concealed side flow to the support hidden behind the firebreak.\n9. **The cold open completed:** he gives up the structure immediately and races for Moss, but the established sit routine and fire timing make him too late.\n10. **Grief becomes a new objective:** he traces the block path, understands the ruin and decides to rebuild the portal for another attempt.\n11. **The builder revealed:** every opening detail is created through visible mining and placement. No speech or second avatar is needed.\n12. **The portal nobody made:** the creeper kills him; altered lava geometry lights the frame; the final match cut returns to his earlier hand entering it. A possible final line is: **‘Nobody had built it before I found it. I built it afterwards.’**\n\nThe narrator may speak retrospectively but cannot conceal knowledge he already has within a scene. The cold open withholds an outcome by cutting away; it does not state a false result.\n\n## Plot and causality\n\nThe major actions form one traceable chain:\n\n- Exclusive authorship and an unvisited map region make the constructed ruin and active portal intolerable contradictions, so the protagonist enters.\n- Entry strands him in the same ravine before any construction, so a temporary survival camp becomes necessary.\n- Surviving together creates the relationship with Moss and produces the cabin whose remains he remembers.\n- Terrain and efficient Minecraft building choices reproduce distinctive shapes from the ruin, allowing recognition through environment rather than another avatar.\n- Recognition causes an active prevention plan: leave with Moss, interrupt the wooden route and neutralise the lava first.\n- Sitting Moss keeps the wolf away from the lava operation but removes autonomous escape when the hidden side flow reaches timber.\n- Mining the cooled lava both supplies the future portal and exposes the fire source.\n- Moss’s death causes the protagonist to abandon his departure plan and construct the remembered portal immediately.\n- The improvised ignition, washed-away torch and dark branch create the conditions for the creeper encounter.\n- The explosion kills the builder and changes the lava geometry; ordinary fire spread then activates the portal found at the beginning.\n\nThere is no moment in which the protagonist debates saving stored blocks instead of Moss. Once Moss is threatened, every action is directed towards the wolf. The tragedy comes from timing, prior choices and Minecraft mechanics, not an implausible value decision.\n\nRemoving the relationship scenes makes the death merely functional. Removing the remembered ruin removes the reason for prevention. Removing the hidden support makes the fire arbitrary. Removing the sit routine permits a convenient escape. Removing the grief-driven portal decision breaks the title reveal. Removing the creeper and lava geometry leaves the initial activation unexplained.\n\n## Characters and relationship\n\n### The protagonist\n\nHe begins as a capable solo player who trusts observation, efficient building and preparation. His first goal is to identify the impossible portal and return to his own time. After becoming stranded, he creates a home and a relationship rather than treating the past as a puzzle room. Once the ruin matches his work, he believes enough careful construction can turn foreknowledge into control.\n\nHis strength is practical attention. His limitation is that grief makes the next technically possible action feel like the only tolerable action. After Moss dies, he does not pause to restore lighting, heal fully or obtain a safer ignition tool; he copies the remembered frame because it appears to promise one more attempt.\n\nHis important choices are to enter, remain near the portal site, tame Moss, build around the terrain, leave with Moss, neutralise the lava, mine the final block, abandon the house for the rescue, reconstruct the frame and attempt immediate ignition.\n\n### Moss\n\nMoss is a tamed companion with Minecraft-scale behaviour, not human reasoning. The wolf follows, waits when instructed, accepts food and cannot understand a future fire. Its reliable response to the sit command makes ordinary ravine work safer and later makes the fire lethal.\n\nMoss is never placed in a pen. Its usual sitting place is beside the protagonist’s bed inside their shared cabin. The relationship moves from tentative taming to an easy work rhythm, then ends through a safety habit that cannot adapt without the player reaching it.\n\n### Opposition and pressure\n\nThere is no secret intruder. The active opposition combines the protagonist’s grief-driven future objective with ordinary survival hazards. Before the fire he wants to preserve Moss and leave safely. After the loss he wants to force one more temporal attempt quickly. The creeper has a simple Minecraft goal once it detects him: approach and detonate. Darkness, low health, lost equipment and the protagonist’s focus on the ignition give it the advantage; lighting, distance, a shield or delay would be vulnerabilities, but his chosen haste removes those protections.\n\nThe fixed timeline describes the resulting history; it does not compel decisions or erase alternatives.\n\n## Movement of the story\n\n| Movement | Temporary objective and strategy | Reversal | Changed state |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1. Effects before causes | Survive the cold-open fragment, inspect the burned ravine and test the impossible portal | The portal opens onto the same place before any construction | Mystery becomes displacement |\n| 2. A home in borrowed time | Establish shelter, resources and a safe routine with Moss | Practical building choices reproduce the ruined foundation | Temporary survival becomes threatened attachment |\n| 3. Outbuild the future | Prepare to leave, interrupt the timber route and neutralise the lava | Mining the hazard opens a concealed flow to hidden wood | Prevention becomes the cause of immediate danger |\n| 4. Too late for the room | Reach Moss without spending time on the structure | The wolf dies just before release, completing the cold open | Protection becomes grief and causal recognition |\n| 5. Make the impossible frame | Rebuild and ignite the portal for another chance | A creeper kills him, but the blast enables lava and fire to activate the frame | Failed escape becomes the opening mystery |\n\n## Scene progression\n\n| Scene | Entry and objective | Visual conflict or clue | Turn and outcome | Production need |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1. Already late | Reach Moss through the burning cabin | Water passes the ignored chest; Moss waits beyond flame | Cut before the interaction | Short controlled fire fragment using real movement |\n| 2. A ruin with no history | Cross an unvisited ravine before night | L-shaped stone shell, granite notch and fire-damaged stair | Portal light appears below | Burned state and map-edge context |\n| 3. The active frame | Explain the frame without inventing an owner | Blast cavity, exposed lava, cobblestone foot and plank remnant | He enters | Portal hero set and transition |\n| 4. Before the blocks | Determine whether this is another place | Same natural landmarks; every player block is absent | He chooses to camp | Pristine geography matched to Scene 2 |\n| 5. Moss | Survive, tame the wolf and establish a routine | Bones are earned from a skeleton; Moss follows normal commands | Temporary shelter becomes shared home | Real taming, feeding, following and building capture |\n| 6. The foundation remembers | Compare the cabin with the opening ruin | Granite seam, chest recess and stair rib align | He recognises the ruin as his future | Matched lenses and block coordinates |\n| 7. Leave at dawn | Make Moss safe and remove the apparent fire route | Night, exposed lava and one concealed timber support complicate the plan | He sits Moss and begins neutralising lava | Legible shared room, no enclosure |\n| 8. The block underneath | Cool and mine the lava shelf | A side source escapes behind the stone-clad stair | Hidden support ignites | Testable water, lava and support geometry |\n| 9. The room from the opening | Reach Moss immediately | Separated fire blocks and the sit state consume seconds | Moss dies before release | Staged fire progression and reliable wolf blocking |\n| 10. Follow the missing blocks | Understand what happened and find another attempt | The burn path explains the opening ruin and supplies obsidian | He chooses to rebuild the frame | Burned reset and visible causal trace |\n| 11. The builder | Assemble and light the remembered portal | No flint and steel, washed-away torch, dark branch and improvised lava cradle | Creeper detonates and kills him | Real construction plus controlled mob/death capture |\n| 12. Nobody made it yet | Show the consequence of the failed ignition | Blast opens lava to the remaining plank; fire reaches the frame | Portal activates and matches the opening | Version-tested ignition and exact match cut |\n\n## Environmental clue design\n\n| Early detail | Ordinary initial reading | Later cause and pay-off |\n|---|---|---|\n| L-shaped cobblestone foundation around granite | Efficient use of a damaged ledge | The protagonist chooses the only flat footprint and builds the remembered ruin |\n| Stone chest recess survives the fire | A protected storage niche | He creates it during the Moss building sequence |\n| Cobblestone foot beneath the portal | Rough repair or incomplete frame | Water cooling during obsidian work creates the exact block |\n| Blast cavity beside exposed lava | Recent environmental damage | The lethal creeper blast breaks the lava-retaining lip |\n| One remaining spruce block near the frame | Debris from the burned stair | It is part of the emergency ignition cradle that catches after the blast |\n| Dark lateral branch | Unimportant cave depth | Rescue water removed its torch, allowing a creeper to approach from darkness |\n| Stone-clad interruption in the stair | A successful firebreak | A continuous spruce support hidden behind the cladding lets fire bypass it |\n| Moss sitting beside the bed | Calm domestic routine | Sitting prevents self-evacuation during the fire |\n| No body or meaningful dropped token | The builder’s fate is unknown | The protagonist dies beside lava and ordinary drops are destroyed |\n| Identical final portal composition | The frame may have always existed | The protagonist creates every physical detail after remembering it |\n\nNo clue depends on footprints, written messages, a silhouette, a second voice or an item that Minecraft would not ordinarily provide.\n\n## Ending and meaning\n\nThe informational and emotional climaxes are the same causal event. Watching the protagonist build the opening frame answers the title question. Watching the creeper kill him while his failed ignition succeeds explains why the frame was active and why no builder remained.\n\nThe ending is tragic because the protagonist does choose Moss over the structure and still arrives too late. His failure is not moral indifference; it is the accumulated result of a sensible safety routine, an incomplete firebreak, a hazardous resource operation and a grief-driven refusal to pause. The proposed thematic argument is that foreknowledge does not automatically become control, and that grief can turn a possible tool into an instruction one feels unable to refuse.\n\nThe final image is the lit portal alone beside the blast cavity. A match cut brings back the earlier protagonist’s hand entering it. The physical loop closes, while the first origin of the portal’s temporal routing remains intentionally open. That open question may add reflective residue, but it cannot conceal any cause required for the home, fire, death or activation.\n\n## Minecraft rules and production expression\n\n### Ordinary mechanics intended to carry plot\n\n- A tamed wolf follows when standing and remains where commanded when sitting.\n- Bones used for taming come from an on-screen skeleton encounter.\n- Water cools exposed lava and can wash away a torch.\n- Mining an apparently safe cooled block can expose another lava source behind or below it.\n- Lava can ignite nearby flammable blocks when fire spread is enabled.\n- Fire can travel through a continuous hidden timber element into the cabin.\n- A valid portal can activate when ordinary fire appears inside its frame.\n- A creeper explosion breaks blocks and changes fluid access; it is not itself treated as a source of fire.\n- A close creeper detonation can be terminal in the proposed Hardcore state.\n\n### Speculative canon\n\nThe frame routes one entry from the later ruined ravine to the earlier pristine ravine and ejects the traveller where no standing frame yet exists. This is invented story canon, not a vanilla Minecraft or physics claim. Materials are not bootstrapped: obsidian, cobblestone, spruce, water and bones all have ordinary origins. Only the remembered frame arrangement and temporal routing lack an external first design.\n\n### Capture approach\n\nMost of the film should be acted through real recorded gameplay: exploration, mining, taming, feeding, building, sitting and releasing the wolf, water placement, fire response, portal construction and mob pressure. No simultaneous double-avatar shot is required. The special work is limited to temporal transitions, matched world states, the final observer angle on the death and the match cut that closes the loop.\n\n## Evidence and approval still required\n\nBefore Story lock, production testing must establish the Minecraft edition, version, difficulty, Hardcore presentation and exact coordinates for:\n\n- concealed lava flow after obsidian removal;\n- fire spread from the hidden support to the cabin;\n- water behaviour and torch removal;\n- the time available to reach a seated wolf;\n- reproducible wolf damage and death without invented blocking;\n- creeper approach, blast radius and terminal player damage;\n- blast modification of the lava-retaining lip;\n- lava-to-spruce ignition and fire-based portal activation;\n- destruction or concealment of ordinary item drops;\n- persistence of the active portal until the opening discovery.\n\nIf any candidate behaviour is unreliable, the geometry should change while preserving the causal functions. No audience test, animatic result, fire test, wolf-pathing test or capture validation is claimed here.","files":[{"path":"02-story/story-plan.md","content":"# Story plan — The Portal That Nobody Made\n\n> This is an unlocked Story proposal. It develops the showrunner note without recording a creative lock, audience result or production validation.\n\n## Development status\n\nThis revision replaces the rescue-choice version with a closed-loop tragedy. The protagonist never sees another version of himself. He first encounters the effects of his own future actions as environmental contradictions, enters the portal, builds a life with a tamed wolf in the same ravine at an earlier time, loses the wolf in a fire, and constructs the portal in a desperate attempt to go back again. A creeper kills him during an improvised lava ignition; the blast changes the lava-and-wood geometry, allowing ordinary fire spread to activate the frame. That is the portal his earlier personal self found.\n\nThe wolf’s working name is **Moss**. Moss has no pen or doghouse. The relationship is established through normal play: taming, travelling, gathering materials, feeding, building around an inquisitive companion, and using the sit command only when the protagonist enters the dangerous ravine mine.\n\nThe proposed world is Hardcore so the final death is terminal. The mode, exact Minecraft edition and mechanical chain remain unlocked until human approval and reproducibility testing.\n\n## Audience promise\n\nA lone player exploring an unvisited part of his private world finds a burned cabin and a lit portal that cannot belong there. The portal takes him not to the Nether but to the same untouched ravine several days earlier. As he survives, builds and bonds with a wolf, practical construction decisions begin producing the shapes he remembers from the ruin. The intended pleasure is recognising that every strange block, missing support and blast scar came from a normal action whose meaning was initially hidden.\n\nThe central questions are:\n\n- Who built and lit the portal?\n- Why does the new homestead become the ruin seen before it existed?\n- Can foreknowledge prevent the fire and save Moss?\n\nThe story would break its promise if another player or distant silhouette supplied the mystery, if an invisible force corrected history, if Moss were kept in an invented enclosure, if the protagonist delayed rescue to save replaceable blocks, or if the portal lit merely because an explosion was treated as magical fire.\n\n## Dramatic opening and relationship grounding\n\n### Cold open\n\nThe film opens inside the later fire. The protagonist ignores a chest and burning wall, empties his water bucket towards the room where Moss is sitting, and breaks directly towards the wolf. Moss is visible through flame for a moment. The protagonist reaches forward; the image cuts before the outcome.\n\nA restrained narration line may frame the promise without lying: **‘By the time I understood the portal, I was already late.’**\n\nThis fragment lasts roughly thirty-five seconds. It establishes immediate danger and makes clear that the protagonist is not choosing the house over the wolf.\n\n### Return to the beginning\n\nThe film then moves to the protagonist’s earlier personal experience. During a solo expedition far from his established base, he reaches a ravine whose coordinates and map edge show that he has not visited it before. On the west rim stands the cobblestone remainder of a burned cabin. Its wooden upper structure is mostly absent. A stair descends towards a north-wall cave, where an active obsidian frame stands beside a fresh blast cavity, exposed lava and the remains of a plank ignition cradle.\n\nThere is no body, dropped collar, sign, book or glimpse of another avatar. The evidence is construction and damage. The protagonist inspects the blocks, confirms that the portal is real, and chooses to enter.\n\n### Building the bond\n\nThe portal ejects him into the same cave several days earlier. The frame, cabin and player-placed stair do not exist. With night approaching and no return portal, he makes a temporary shelter on the only level shelf above the ravine.\n\nA skeleton encounter supplies bones through ordinary gameplay. He uses them to tame a nearby wolf and calls it Moss. The shelter then grows into a compact spruce-and-cobblestone cabin through a short series of lived scenes rather than a decorative montage alone:\n\n- Moss follows while the protagonist gathers spruce and repeatedly occupies the next block position during construction.\n- The protagonist feeds Moss after ordinary damage and waits for the tail to rise before continuing.\n- They return together at dusk; the protagonist closes the door, places food in the furnace and sits Moss beside the bed only before descending towards lava.\n- On returning from the mine, he releases Moss immediately, and the wolf follows him through the next round of chores.\n- A repeated wide shot records the cabin, the ravine stair and the two of them crossing the west ledge together.\n\nThese scenes give the later sit command emotional and mechanical meaning. It is a familiar safety routine, not confinement.\n\n## Story in world chronology\n\n1. The protagonist’s pre-travel self is several days away on a solo expedition. His later travelling self is ejected into the untouched ravine through a temporary portal plane. The two versions remain geographically separate and never see one another.\n2. The travelling protagonist camps on the west shelf, tames Moss with locally obtained bones and gradually builds the spruce-and-cobblestone cabin, stone chest recess and ravine stair later seen as ruins.\n3. Terrain dictates several distinctive decisions: the foundation bends around a granite seam, the stair anchors to a narrow stone rib, and one stripped-spruce support passes behind a stone-clad section above the lava shelf.\n4. The protagonist recognises those shapes from the ruin he saw before entering the portal. He decides to leave with Moss at dawn. Before leaving, he tries to neutralise the lava beneath the stair and collect enough obsidian for a controlled return frame.\n5. Moss is seated beside the bed because the lava shelf is dangerous. The protagonist pours water over the visible lava and mines the cooled blocks. Removing the final accessible block opens a concealed lateral lava flow. Water cools the face he can see, but the side flow reaches the hidden spruce support behind the stone cladding.\n6. Fire travels through that continuous support and into separated parts of the cabin. The protagonist immediately abandons the structure, routes his single water source towards Moss and breaks through the shortest wall. He reaches the sitting place one damage interval too late. Moss dies in the fire; no collar or invented keepsake drops.\n7. Following the missing-block path back down reveals that the fire, the surviving stone shell and the opening ruin are the same event. Grief converts the portal from a mystery into a proposed second chance.\n8. Using the obsidian he mined, the protagonist builds the exact economical frame in the north-wall cave. The water-cooled cobblestone foot, asymmetry and remaining plank position reproduce the details from the opening.\n9. His flint and steel and spare iron were lost with the cabin. Rather than pause to mine and smelt, he attempts an ordinary emergency ignition: lava outside the frame, with spruce positioned so a fire block may form inside it. Water used during the rescue has washed a torch from a lateral branch, leaving an approach dark.\n10. A creeper wanders from that branch while the protagonist waits for the ignition. At close range it detonates and kills the already damaged protagonist. The explosion does not directly create fire. It breaks the cobblestone lip retaining the placed lava and removes part of the cradle. Lava reaches the remaining spruce; a subsequent fire spread places flame inside the valid frame and activates it.\n11. The protagonist’s drops fall into lava or fire. The portal remains active beside the blast cavity. Later in world chronology, his earlier personal self reaches the ravine, finds the burned cabin and enters that same portal, beginning the journey just described.\n\n## Personal journey and audience presentation\n\nThe film uses a cold-open fragment, then follows the protagonist’s personal order:\n\n1. **Fire without context:** a brief image of the attempted rescue ends before Moss’s fate is known.\n2. **The ruin before the home:** the protagonist explores the burned cabin and reads its surviving construction without knowing it is his work.\n3. **The impossible threshold:** the active portal, blast cavity, cobblestone foot, exposed lava and incomplete ignition cradle are inspected. He enters by choice.\n4. **The ravine before construction:** the same geography is pristine and the portal frame is absent. Persistent natural landmarks establish an earlier state rather than another world.\n5. **A life made in the gap:** survival play, taming and building establish Moss and the cabin as lived experience.\n6. **Recognition through design:** the granite notch, foundation bend, chest recess and stair rib reproduce the remembered ruin. The protagonist accepts that he saw the future of this home.\n7. **A practical prevention plan:** he chooses departure with Moss, stone-clads the stair and attempts to remove the lava hazard before dawn.\n8. **Prevention becomes cause:** obsidian mining releases the concealed side flow to the support hidden behind the firebreak.\n9. **The cold open completed:** he gives up the structure immediately and races for Moss, but the established sit routine and fire timing make him too late.\n10. **Grief becomes a new objective:** he traces the block path, understands the ruin and decides to rebuild the portal for another attempt.\n11. **The builder revealed:** every opening detail is created through visible mining and placement. No speech or second avatar is needed.\n12. **The portal nobody made:** the creeper kills him; altered lava geometry lights the frame; the final match cut returns to his earlier hand entering it. A possible final line is: **‘Nobody had built it before I found it. I built it afterwards.’**\n\nThe narrator may speak retrospectively but cannot conceal knowledge he already has within a scene. The cold open withholds an outcome by cutting away; it does not state a false result.\n\n## Plot and causality\n\nThe major actions form one traceable chain:\n\n- Exclusive authorship and an unvisited map region make the constructed ruin and active portal intolerable contradictions, so the protagonist enters.\n- Entry strands him in the same ravine before any construction, so a temporary survival camp becomes necessary.\n- Surviving together creates the relationship with Moss and produces the cabin whose remains he remembers.\n- Terrain and efficient Minecraft building choices reproduce distinctive shapes from the ruin, allowing recognition through environment rather than another avatar.\n- Recognition causes an active prevention plan: leave with Moss, interrupt the wooden route and neutralise the lava first.\n- Sitting Moss keeps the wolf away from the lava operation but removes autonomous escape when the hidden side flow reaches timber.\n- Mining the cooled lava both supplies the future portal and exposes the fire source.\n- Moss’s death causes the protagonist to abandon his departure plan and construct the remembered portal immediately.\n- The improvised ignition, washed-away torch and dark branch create the conditions for the creeper encounter.\n- The explosion kills the builder and changes the lava geometry; ordinary fire spread then activates the portal found at the beginning.\n\nThere is no moment in which the protagonist debates saving stored blocks instead of Moss. Once Moss is threatened, every action is directed towards the wolf. The tragedy comes from timing, prior choices and Minecraft mechanics, not an implausible value decision.\n\nRemoving the relationship scenes makes the death merely functional. Removing the remembered ruin removes the reason for prevention. Removing the hidden support makes the fire arbitrary. Removing the sit routine permits a convenient escape. Removing the grief-driven portal decision breaks the title reveal. Removing the creeper and lava geometry leaves the initial activation unexplained.\n\n## Characters and relationship\n\n### The protagonist\n\nHe begins as a capable solo player who trusts observation, efficient building and preparation. His first goal is to identify the impossible portal and return to his own time. After becoming stranded, he creates a home and a relationship rather than treating the past as a puzzle room. Once the ruin matches his work, he believes enough careful construction can turn foreknowledge into control.\n\nHis strength is practical attention. His limitation is that grief makes the next technically possible action feel like the only tolerable action. After Moss dies, he does not pause to restore lighting, heal fully or obtain a safer ignition tool; he copies the remembered frame because it appears to promise one more attempt.\n\nHis important choices are to enter, remain near the portal site, tame Moss, build around the terrain, leave with Moss, neutralise the lava, mine the final block, abandon the house for the rescue, reconstruct the frame and attempt immediate ignition.\n\n### Moss\n\nMoss is a tamed companion with Minecraft-scale behaviour, not human reasoning. The wolf follows, waits when instructed, accepts food and cannot understand a future fire. Its reliable response to the sit command makes ordinary ravine work safer and later makes the fire lethal.\n\nMoss is never placed in a pen. Its usual sitting place is beside the protagonist’s bed inside their shared cabin. The relationship moves from tentative taming to an easy work rhythm, then ends through a safety habit that cannot adapt without the player reaching it.\n\n### Opposition and pressure\n\nThere is no secret intruder. The active opposition combines the protagonist’s grief-driven future objective with ordinary survival hazards. Before the fire he wants to preserve Moss and leave safely. After the loss he wants to force one more temporal attempt quickly. The creeper has a simple Minecraft goal once it detects him: approach and detonate. Darkness, low health, lost equipment and the protagonist’s focus on the ignition give it the advantage; lighting, distance, a shield or delay would be vulnerabilities, but his chosen haste removes those protections.\n\nThe fixed timeline describes the resulting history; it does not compel decisions or erase alternatives.\n\n## Movement of the story\n\n| Movement | Temporary objective and strategy | Reversal | Changed state |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1. Effects before causes | Survive the cold-open fragment, inspect the burned ravine and test the impossible portal | The portal opens onto the same place before any construction | Mystery becomes displacement |\n| 2. A home in borrowed time | Establish shelter, resources and a safe routine with Moss | Practical building choices reproduce the ruined foundation | Temporary survival becomes threatened attachment |\n| 3. Outbuild the future | Prepare to leave, interrupt the timber route and neutralise the lava | Mining the hazard opens a concealed flow to hidden wood | Prevention becomes the cause of immediate danger |\n| 4. Too late for the room | Reach Moss without spending time on the structure | The wolf dies just before release, completing the cold open | Protection becomes grief and causal recognition |\n| 5. Make the impossible frame | Rebuild and ignite the portal for another chance | A creeper kills him, but the blast enables lava and fire to activate the frame | Failed escape becomes the opening mystery |\n\n## Scene progression\n\n| Scene | Entry and objective | Visual conflict or clue | Turn and outcome | Production need |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1. Already late | Reach Moss through the burning cabin | Water passes the ignored chest; Moss waits beyond flame | Cut before the interaction | Short controlled fire fragment using real movement |\n| 2. A ruin with no history | Cross an unvisited ravine before night | L-shaped stone shell, granite notch and fire-damaged stair | Portal light appears below | Burned state and map-edge context |\n| 3. The active frame | Explain the frame without inventing an owner | Blast cavity, exposed lava, cobblestone foot and plank remnant | He enters | Portal hero set and transition |\n| 4. Before the blocks | Determine whether this is another place | Same natural landmarks; every player block is absent | He chooses to camp | Pristine geography matched to Scene 2 |\n| 5. Moss | Survive, tame the wolf and establish a routine | Bones are earned from a skeleton; Moss follows normal commands | Temporary shelter becomes shared home | Real taming, feeding, following and building capture |\n| 6. The foundation remembers | Compare the cabin with the opening ruin | Granite seam, chest recess and stair rib align | He recognises the ruin as his future | Matched lenses and block coordinates |\n| 7. Leave at dawn | Make Moss safe and remove the apparent fire route | Night, exposed lava and one concealed timber support complicate the plan | He sits Moss and begins neutralising lava | Legible shared room, no enclosure |\n| 8. The block underneath | Cool and mine the lava shelf | A side source escapes behind the stone-clad stair | Hidden support ignites | Testable water, lava and support geometry |\n| 9. The room from the opening | Reach Moss immediately | Separated fire blocks and the sit state consume seconds | Moss dies before release | Staged fire progression and reliable wolf blocking |\n| 10. Follow the missing blocks | Understand what happened and find another attempt | The burn path explains the opening ruin and supplies obsidian | He chooses to rebuild the frame | Burned reset and visible causal trace |\n| 11. The builder | Assemble and light the remembered portal | No flint and steel, washed-away torch, dark branch and improvised lava cradle | Creeper detonates and kills him | Real construction plus controlled mob/death capture |\n| 12. Nobody made it yet | Show the consequence of the failed ignition | Blast opens lava to the remaining plank; fire reaches the frame | Portal activates and matches the opening | Version-tested ignition and exact match cut |\n\n## Environmental clue design\n\n| Early detail | Ordinary initial reading | Later cause and pay-off |\n|---|---|---|\n| L-shaped cobblestone foundation around granite | Efficient use of a damaged ledge | The protagonist chooses the only flat footprint and builds the remembered ruin |\n| Stone chest recess survives the fire | A protected storage niche | He creates it during the Moss building sequence |\n| Cobblestone foot beneath the portal | Rough repair or incomplete frame | Water cooling during obsidian work creates the exact block |\n| Blast cavity beside exposed lava | Recent environmental damage | The lethal creeper blast breaks the lava-retaining lip |\n| One remaining spruce block near the frame | Debris from the burned stair | It is part of the emergency ignition cradle that catches after the blast |\n| Dark lateral branch | Unimportant cave depth | Rescue water removed its torch, allowing a creeper to approach from darkness |\n| Stone-clad interruption in the stair | A successful firebreak | A continuous spruce support hidden behind the cladding lets fire bypass it |\n| Moss sitting beside the bed | Calm domestic routine | Sitting prevents self-evacuation during the fire |\n| No body or meaningful dropped token | The builder’s fate is unknown | The protagonist dies beside lava and ordinary drops are destroyed |\n| Identical final portal composition | The frame may have always existed | The protagonist creates every physical detail after remembering it |\n\nNo clue depends on footprints, written messages, a silhouette, a second voice or an item that Minecraft would not ordinarily provide.\n\n## Ending and meaning\n\nThe informational and emotional climaxes are the same causal event. Watching the protagonist build the opening frame answers the title question. Watching the creeper kill him while his failed ignition succeeds explains why the frame was active and why no builder remained.\n\nThe ending is tragic because the protagonist does choose Moss over the structure and still arrives too late. His failure is not moral indifference; it is the accumulated result of a sensible safety routine, an incomplete firebreak, a hazardous resource operation and a grief-driven refusal to pause. The proposed thematic argument is that foreknowledge does not automatically become control, and that grief can turn a possible tool into an instruction one feels unable to refuse.\n\nThe final image is the lit portal alone beside the blast cavity. A match cut brings back the earlier protagonist’s hand entering it. The physical loop closes, while the first origin of the portal’s temporal routing remains intentionally open. That open question may add reflective residue, but it cannot conceal any cause required for the home, fire, death or activation.\n\n## Minecraft rules and production expression\n\n### Ordinary mechanics intended to carry plot\n\n- A tamed wolf follows when standing and remains where commanded when sitting.\n- Bones used for taming come from an on-screen skeleton encounter.\n- Water cools exposed lava and can wash away a torch.\n- Mining an apparently safe cooled block can expose another lava source behind or below it.\n- Lava can ignite nearby flammable blocks when fire spread is enabled.\n- Fire can travel through a continuous hidden timber element into the cabin.\n- A valid portal can activate when ordinary fire appears inside its frame.\n- A creeper explosion breaks blocks and changes fluid access; it is not itself treated as a source of fire.\n- A close creeper detonation can be terminal in the proposed Hardcore state.\n\n### Speculative canon\n\nThe frame routes one entry from the later ruined ravine to the earlier pristine ravine and ejects the traveller where no standing frame yet exists. This is invented story canon, not a vanilla Minecraft or physics claim. Materials are not bootstrapped: obsidian, cobblestone, spruce, water and bones all have ordinary origins. Only the remembered frame arrangement and temporal routing lack an external first design.\n\n### Capture approach\n\nMost of the film should be acted through real recorded gameplay: exploration, mining, taming, feeding, building, sitting and releasing the wolf, water placement, fire response, portal construction and mob pressure. No simultaneous double-avatar shot is required. The special work is limited to temporal transitions, matched world states, the final observer angle on the death and the match cut that closes the loop.\n\n## Evidence and approval still required\n\nBefore Story lock, production testing must establish the Minecraft edition, version, difficulty, Hardcore presentation and exact coordinates for:\n\n- concealed lava flow after obsidian removal;\n- fire spread from the hidden support to the cabin;\n- water behaviour and torch removal;\n- the time available to reach a seated wolf;\n- reproducible wolf damage and death without invented blocking;\n- creeper approach, blast radius and terminal player damage;\n- blast modification of the lava-retaining lip;\n- lava-to-spruce ignition and fire-based portal activation;\n- destruction or concealment of ordinary item drops;\n- persistence of the active portal until the opening discovery.\n\nIf any candidate behaviour is unreliable, the geometry should change while preserving the causal functions. No audience test, animatic result, fire test, wolf-pathing test or capture validation is claimed here."},{"path":".studio/internal/story/story-model.json","content":"{\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"audience_promise\": {\n    \"novel_situation\": \"A lone Hardcore player finds a burned cabin and an active portal in an unvisited ravine, enters it, and is thrown back to the untouched place where he will build the cabin, tame a wolf and eventually create the portal himself.\",\n    \"human_problem\": \"Foreknowledge convinces him that careful preparation can protect Moss, but after the wolf dies his grief turns a possible return mechanism into an urgent compulsion.\",\n    \"pressure_source\": \"A remembered ruin, a concealed lava path, a seated wolf, lost equipment, a dark cave branch and an approaching creeper compress his choices through ordinary Minecraft conditions.\",\n    \"expected_pleasure\": [\n      \"Recognising future ruins in practical building decisions\",\n      \"Reinterpreting blast damage, block gaps and portal asymmetry as consequences of normal play\",\n      \"Watching the protagonist and Moss develop an ordinary working relationship before danger\",\n      \"Discovering that the failed attempt to light the portal is what activates the portal from the opening\"\n    ],\n    \"disappointment_test\": \"The promise fails if a visible other player supplies the mystery, the timeline invisibly corrects events, Moss is kept in an invented pen, the protagonist chooses blocks over the wolf, the fire lacks a readable block path, or the creeper explosion is falsely treated as direct fire generation.\"\n  },\n  \"premise\": {\n    \"disruption\": \"In an unvisited region of his private world, the protagonist finds a burned player-built cabin and a complete active portal beside fresh blast damage.\",\n    \"protagonist_id\": \"PROTAGONIST\",\n    \"difficult_goal\": \"Understand the portal, survive its one-way displacement and prevent the future ruin from taking Moss.\",\n    \"central_opposition\": \"His grief-driven later objective conflicts with his earlier safety plan while lava, fire, wolf behaviour, darkness and a creeper make delay consequential.\",\n    \"meaningful_consequence\": \"His attempt to neutralise the future fire exposes its hidden path; after Moss dies, his attempt to return creates and activates the portal that began the journey.\",\n    \"logline\": \"After an impossible portal throws a lone Minecraft player into the past of the same ravine, he builds a home and bonds with a wolf, only to discover that his efforts to prevent a remembered fire will create it; when the wolf dies, his desperate return portal becomes both his grave and the mystery his earlier self will find.\"\n  },\n  \"theme\": {\n    \"subject\": \"Foreknowledge, grief and the limits of control\",\n    \"question\": \"When knowledge of a loss cannot guarantee control, does acting faster protect what matters or complete the pattern one fears?\",\n    \"provisional_argument\": \"Foreknowledge is not control, and grief can make a technically possible action feel compulsory even when care would require pause, uncertainty or departure.\",\n    \"competing_values\": [\n      \"Care expressed through present attention\",\n      \"Control pursued through reversal\",\n      \"Patient safety and incomplete knowledge\",\n      \"Urgent action and the promise of another chance\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"characters\": [\n    {\n      \"character_id\": \"PROTAGONIST\",\n      \"role\": \"protagonist\",\n      \"want\": \"Identify the portal, return to his proper time and keep Moss from becoming part of the burned future.\",\n      \"need\": \"Recognise that technical possibility does not make immediate action safe or loss reversible.\",\n      \"fear\": \"That the ruined cabin is evidence of a future he will reproduce regardless of his skill.\",\n      \"belief\": \"If he can map every surviving block and remove the apparent hazard, foreknowledge will let him control the outcome.\",\n      \"limitation\": \"After Moss dies, grief narrows his attention until reconstructing the portal feels more urgent than healing, relighting the cave or obtaining a safer ignition tool.\",\n      \"strategy\": \"Inspect persistent geography, build efficiently around terrain, establish a stone firebreak, neutralise the lava shelf and reconstruct the remembered portal from local materials.\",\n      \"contradiction\": \"His patience creates a genuine home with Moss, but his later urgency sacrifices the same caution that made that life possible.\",\n      \"boundary\": \"Once Moss is threatened, he will abandon every block and stored resource to reach the wolf.\",\n      \"pressure_point\": \"The hidden support ignites while Moss is seated inside and the protagonist is below the cabin with one water source.\",\n      \"relationship_function\": \"Moss turns the portal from an abstract authorship puzzle into a life the protagonist has made and cannot accept losing.\",\n      \"agency\": \"He enters the portal, camps at the ravine, tames Moss, chooses the cabin footprint, prepares to leave, neutralises and mines the lava, races directly to Moss, rebuilds the frame and attempts immediate lava ignition.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"character_id\": \"WOLF\",\n      \"role\": \"companion\",\n      \"want\": \"Expressed behaviourally, to follow the protagonist when standing and remain near the shared home.\",\n      \"need\": \"The protagonist must reach and release it from sitting before fire reaches the room.\",\n      \"fear\": \"Expressed behaviourally through distress and fire damage rather than humanised thought.\",\n      \"belief\": \"No abstract belief is assigned; its trained behaviour is to wait when seated and follow when released.\",\n      \"limitation\": \"It cannot interpret the ruined future, assess the firebreak or cancel a sit command.\",\n      \"strategy\": \"Follow during ordinary work, accept food and wait beside the bed during dangerous ravine mining.\",\n      \"contradiction\": \"The obedience that keeps it away from lava during normal play prevents autonomous evacuation during the fire.\",\n      \"boundary\": \"It is not placed in a pen, given human reasoning or used to solve the temporal mystery.\",\n      \"pressure_point\": \"Fire reaches its familiar sitting place before the protagonist can cross the burning route and interact.\",\n      \"relationship_function\": \"Moss makes the stranded interval a life rather than a puzzle and gives the protagonist’s final attempt its tragic motive.\",\n      \"agency\": \"Limited diegetic agency: Moss follows, obstructs building, accepts food and responds to commands, but cannot override the final sit state.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"opposition\": {\n    \"opposing_goal\": \"After Moss dies, the protagonist’s grief-driven self seeks to activate the remembered portal immediately; once detected, the creeper seeks to approach and detonate before that attempt is safe.\",\n    \"why_neither_side_can_withdraw\": \"The protagonist treats the portal as the only remaining route to Moss before the fire, while the creeper’s ordinary hostile behaviour continues once it has acquired him at close range.\",\n    \"advantage\": \"The portal memory supplies an exact tempting plan, the protagonist has lost safer tools, rescue water has darkened the branch, and close cave geometry favours the creeper.\",\n    \"vulnerability\": \"The protagonist could relight the branch, heal, replace his shield, mine iron or abandon immediate ignition; the creeper is vulnerable to light, distance and prepared defence.\",\n    \"cost_of_confrontation\": \"Stopping to make the cave safe means accepting Moss’s death long enough to delay the apparent second chance.\",\n    \"cost_of_avoidance\": \"Continuing in haste leaves the protagonist exposed, creates the lethal blast geometry and completes the portal found at the beginning.\"\n  },\n  \"stakes\": {\n    \"immediate\": \"Survive the one-way displacement and determine whether the ravine is the same place at another time.\",\n    \"practical\": \"The cabin, stored resources, safe stair and only known temporal route can be destroyed.\",\n    \"personal\": \"The protagonist’s confidence that careful play can master every visible system is dismantled.\",\n    \"relational\": \"Moss will remain seated in the burning room unless the protagonist reaches it in time.\",\n    \"moral\": \"The tragedy must not arise from preferring property over the wolf; it arises from prior safety choices and grief-driven haste.\",\n    \"existential\": \"The protagonist learns that remembering an effect does not grant freedom from becoming its cause.\",\n    \"systemic\": \"The consequences remain confined to one solo world, one ravine, one player and one wolf.\"\n  },\n  \"story_layers\": {\n    \"chronological_story\": \"The travelling protagonist arrives in the ravine while his pre-travel self is far away, builds a cabin, tames Moss, exposes a lava path that causes the fire, loses Moss, builds the portal and dies in a creeper blast that enables its activation; later his earlier personal self reaches the ruin and enters that portal.\",\n    \"causal_plot\": \"The impossible portal prompts entry; displacement prompts shelter; shelter and taming create attachment; remembered ruins prompt prevention; prevention exposes lava; the sit routine and fire timing cause loss; loss prompts portal construction; unsafe ignition and darkness enable the creeper; the blast enables fire to activate the opening frame.\",\n    \"audience_presentation\": \"A fragment of the failed rescue opens the film, followed by portal discovery, displacement, relationship building, environmental recognition, prevention, completed loss, construction, death and a final match cut to the opening entry.\"\n  },\n  \"ending_design\": {\n    \"final_choice\": \"Reconstruct and attempt to ignite the remembered frame immediately rather than pause to heal, restore lighting and obtain a safe ignition tool.\",\n    \"climax_mechanism\": \"The protagonist places a lava-and-spruce emergency ignition; a creeper from the dark branch kills him and breaks the retaining lip, after which lava ignites the remaining spruce and ordinary fire activates the valid portal.\",\n    \"emotional_result\": \"The builder mystery resolves at the same moment grief, fatal haste and the closed personal journey become fully legible.\",\n    \"thematic_consequence\": \"The portal offers temporal knowledge but not mastery; the attempt to force another chance becomes the cause of the first chance.\",\n    \"final_relationship_state\": \"Moss is dead and the protagonist’s last construction is motivated by the life they shared rather than by attachment to the lost cabin.\",\n    \"final_image\": \"The active portal stands alone beside the blast cavity before a match cut returns to the earlier protagonist entering it.\",\n    \"unresolved_element\": \"The first external origin of the frame’s temporal routing remains intentionally open and does not substitute for any practical cause.\"\n  },\n  \"minecraft_story_engine\": {\n    \"persistent_blocks\": \"The foundation bend, granite notch, chest recess, cobblestone portal foot, hidden support gap, blast cavity and remaining ignition plank are effects shown before their causes.\",\n    \"repeatable_geography\": \"One west-rim shelf, north-wall cave, lava shelf, stone rib and lateral dark branch remain recognisable across pristine, built, burning and ruined states.\",\n    \"resource_constraints\": \"Bones, spruce, cobblestone, water and obsidian have local origins; one water source and lost iron constrain the fire response and final ignition.\",\n    \"mob_and_animal_behaviour\": \"Moss follows ordinary sit and follow states; a skeleton supplies taming bones; a creeper approaches from darkness and detonates at close range.\",\n    \"portal_rule\": \"The temporal route is invented canon, while the physical frame, lava ignition and fire activation are intended to use ordinary Minecraft behaviours.\",\n    \"production_expression\": \"The design avoids duplicate-avatar shots and relies mainly on recorded exploration, taming, building, mining, water, fire, wolf interaction and mob gameplay.\"\n  },\n  \"narrative_decisions\": {\n    \"structure_framework\": \"A closed-loop tragedy organised by causal revelation and character consequence, not by a mandatory beat formula.\",\n    \"timeline_model\": \"One self-consistent world history with the protagonist’s personal order crossing from a later ruin to an earlier pristine ravine.\",\n    \"opening_strategy\": \"A short honest fire fragment supplies immediate danger before the portal discovery and early relationship-building movement.\",\n    \"mystery_strategy\": \"No other self is seen; identity and chronology are communicated through environment, materials, damage and matched actions.\",\n    \"protagonist_overlap\": \"The pre-travel and travelling versions exist at distant locations for part of world chronology but never meet, see or hear one another.\",\n    \"relationship_strategy\": \"Moss shares the cabin and ordinary work routine; no pen, doghouse or symbolic confinement is used.\",\n    \"fire_and_death_chain\": \"Obsidian mining exposes lateral lava to a hidden support; later a creeper blast changes a separate lava ignition geometry and kills the protagonist.\",\n    \"ending_principle\": \"The wolf and protagonist both die, and the failed return attempt creates the active portal from the opening.\",\n    \"narration_policy\": \"Narration may withhold by viewpoint or cut but may not lie, assert unobserved mechanics or imply another player.\",\n    \"scope_choice\": \"Approximately twenty minutes, one narrator, one avatar, one wolf, one ravine complex and four reusable environment states.\"\n  }\n}"},{"path":".studio/internal/story/causal-spine.csv","content":"event_id,order,summary,cause_ids,cause_type,actor_id,goal,decision,consequence,state_change,new_information,audience_effect,dependent_event_ids,counterfactual_result,deletion_impact,production_note\n\"CE-01\",\"1\",\"On a solo Hardcore expedition, the protagonist reaches an unvisited ravine containing a burned cabin and an active portal.\",\"\",\"initial_state\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"Understand the impossible construction before night forces him away.\",\"He inspects the persistent block evidence instead of treating the ruin as scenery.\",\"The portal becomes an actionable contradiction rather than an unexplained background object.\",\"Routine exploration becomes authorship mystery.\",\"The cabin and frame were deliberately built in a region he has not previously visited.\",\"Immediate danger from the cold open is joined by a fair environmental puzzle.\",\"CE-02|CE-05\",\"pass\",\"Without the authored ruin, entering the portal lacks mystery and later construction has nothing precise to fulfil.\",\"Show map-edge or coordinate context, a burned stone shell and no second avatar or invented evidence.\"\n\"CE-02\",\"2\",\"He chooses to enter the active portal beside the blast cavity.\",\"CE-01\",\"prior_event\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"Test the only object capable of explaining the ruin and temporal contradiction.\",\"He crosses despite having no verified destination or return frame.\",\"He is ejected into the same ravine before the cabin, stair or portal exists.\",\"Investigator becomes temporally stranded survivor.\",\"The portal routes through time rather than to an ordinary Nether destination.\",\"The title mystery expands into questions about when the ruin was made and whether it is his future.\",\"CE-03|CE-08\",\"pass\",\"Deleting entry removes the displacement, relationship, fire, builder reveal and closed journey.\",\"Use one controlled temporal transition and match natural geography exactly.\"\n\"CE-03\",\"3\",\"In the pristine ravine, he chooses to camp near the absent portal site and investigate rather than begin a long blind journey.\",\"CE-02\",\"external_disruption\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"Survive the night and preserve access to the only temporal evidence.\",\"He builds a temporary shelter on the west shelf and gathers local resources.\",\"The practical camp creates the foundation of the later cabin.\",\"Displacement becomes provisional settlement.\",\"Every player-placed block from the opening lies in this location’s future.\",\"Disorientation gives way to cautious possibility.\",\"CE-04\",\"pass\",\"Without settlement, the cabin and relationship never acquire ordinary causes.\",\"Capture the pristine shelf and bare cave before placing any construction.\"\n\"CE-04\",\"4\",\"He tames Moss and turns the temporary shelter into a shared working home.\",\"CE-03\",\"prior_event\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"Create a safe routine while he studies the portal site and gathers supplies.\",\"He uses bones earned from a skeleton, feeds Moss, builds around the terrain and sits the wolf only before dangerous ravine work.\",\"The cabin footprint and sit-follow ritual become durable parts of the world.\",\"Temporary survival becomes relational attachment.\",\"The wolf’s obedience and the cabin’s distinctive geometry arise through normal play.\",\"Warmth and rapport make the remembered ruin emotionally threatening.\",\"CE-05|CE-07\",\"pass\",\"Without the bond and routine, Moss’s death is mechanical leverage rather than earned loss.\",\"Use real taming, feeding, following, building obstruction and return-from-mine actions.\"\n\"CE-05\",\"5\",\"Recognising the opening ruin in his own foundation, he decides to leave with Moss after neutralising the lava and collecting portal material.\",\"CE-01|CE-04\",\"parallel_cause\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"Prevent the remembered fire without abandoning the only route he may be able to reconstruct.\",\"He stone-clads the stair, seats Moss away from the lava and begins cooling and mining the shelf.\",\"A visible firebreak exists, but one continuous stripped-spruce support remains concealed behind it.\",\"Attachment under threat becomes active prevention.\",\"The future ruin is his cabin, and its apparent hazard can be investigated through block continuity.\",\"Recognition produces hope because he acts promptly and sensibly.\",\"CE-06|CE-08\",\"pass\",\"Deleting recognition removes his prevention strategy and makes later fulfilment coincidental.\",\"Match the granite seam, foundation bend, chest recess and stair rib to the opening ruin.\"\n\"CE-06\",\"6\",\"Mining the final accessible obsidian opens a lateral lava flow that reaches the hidden timber support.\",\"CE-05\",\"prior_event\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"Remove the lava hazard and obtain enough obsidian for a controlled return frame.\",\"He keeps water on the visible face and mines the cooled block, accepting the ordinary risk of another source behind it.\",\"The side flow bypasses the visible stone cladding and ignites the continuous support.\",\"Prevention becomes active fire.\",\"The fire begins through ordinary material adjacency created by his own build and mining choices.\",\"Hope reverses into causal dread without invoking supernatural correction.\",\"CE-07\",\"pass\",\"Without the concealed side flow and support, the fire is arbitrary or requires an invented force.\",\"The lava path, ignition distance and timing require target-version reproduction.\"\n\"CE-07\",\"7\",\"He abandons every structural objective and attempts to release Moss, but reaches the sitting place too late.\",\"CE-04|CE-06\",\"parallel_cause\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"Save Moss regardless of the cabin’s destruction.\",\"He directs his single water source towards the wolf, ignores storage and breaks the shortest route through the wall.\",\"Moss dies from fire damage before the release interaction completes; the cabin burns to the opening state.\",\"Protection becomes irreversible grief.\",\"The sit routine and hidden fire route, not a house-versus-wolf choice, caused the loss.\",\"The cold-open question resolves tragically while preserving the protagonist’s moral priority.\",\"CE-08|CE-09\",\"pass\",\"Deleting the failed rescue removes the emotional cause of portal construction and the story’s tragic centre.\",\"Rehearse fire stages, water route, interaction distance and wolf damage; do not claim reliability before testing.\"\n\"CE-08\",\"8\",\"After tracing the burn path, he builds the remembered frame to seek another chance before the fire.\",\"CE-02|CE-07\",\"parallel_cause\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"Use the only temporal mechanism he has witnessed to return to Moss while it is alive.\",\"He rejects departure and immediately reconstructs the economical frame from locally mined obsidian.\",\"The cobblestone foot, asymmetry and ignition position reproduce the portal from the opening.\",\"Grief becomes committed reconstruction.\",\"The unknown builder is the protagonist after Moss’s death.\",\"Mystery satisfaction is inseparable from concern about his haste.\",\"CE-09|CE-10\",\"pass\",\"Without this decision, the title question and closed personal journey remain unanswered.\",\"Repeat the opening portal composition through actual mining and block placement.\"\n\"CE-09\",\"9\",\"While he attempts an emergency lava-and-spruce ignition, a creeper from the dark branch detonates and kills him.\",\"CE-07|CE-08\",\"external_disruption\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"Activate the frame immediately without the flint, steel and spare iron lost in the fire.\",\"He continues the ignition while damaged and without restoring the branch torch or defensive equipment.\",\"The close blast is terminal and breaks the cobblestone lip retaining his placed lava.\",\"Desperate builder becomes absent builder; contained ignition becomes altered fluid geometry.\",\"The opening blast cavity and lack of a builder are consequences of the final attempt.\",\"The apparent accident becomes the earned lethal reversal.\",\"CE-10\",\"pass\",\"Without the creeper event, his absence and the blast geometry beside the opening portal have no cause.\",\"Confirm spawning or approach logic, blast radius, health state and Hardcore presentation in the target build.\"\n\"CE-10\",\"10\",\"The altered lava reaches the remaining spruce, fire forms inside the valid frame and the portal activates for his earlier personal self.\",\"CE-08|CE-09\",\"parallel_cause\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"Create a route back through the ignition arrangement he chose before death.\",\"His prior placement of the lava source, retaining lip and spruce cradle allows the post-blast fire path to complete.\",\"The active portal survives beside burning drops and later becomes the impossible frame encountered at the beginning.\",\"Failed escape becomes self-authored threshold.\",\"The creeper did not directly create fire; it exposed the ordinary lava-and-wood ignition that lit the portal.\",\"The builder, activation, blast cavity and journey close in one visual causal pay-off.\",\"\",\"pass\",\"Deleting activation breaks the opening premise and leaves the frame inert despite every preceding cause.\",\"Test blast-modified flow, fire spread into the frame, item destruction and portal persistence; use a match cut rather than a duplicate-avatar shot.\""},{"path":".studio/internal/story/sequence-outline.csv","content":"sequence_id,order,temporary_goal,strategy,primary_location,complications,reversal,outcome,story_state_afterward,estimated_seconds,production_note\n\"SQ-01\",\"1\",\"Survive the promised fire fragment, then determine what the burned ravine and active portal are.\",\"Open on direct rescue action, cut before the outcome, inspect persistent block evidence and enter when no ordinary destination explains it.\",\"Burning cabin fragment, ruined west rim and north-wall portal cave\",\"The region is unvisited, the cabin is deliberately built, the frame is active and no owner or body exists.\",\"The portal leads to the same pristine ravine before any player construction.\",\"The protagonist crosses by choice and loses his temporal and geographic certainty.\",\"An environmental mystery becomes one-way displacement.\",\"180\",\"Keep the cold open under forty seconds; show no silhouette, written message or second avatar.\"\n\"SQ-02\",\"2\",\"Establish a safe life while preserving access to the absent portal site.\",\"Camp on the west shelf, obtain bones through survival play, tame Moss and expand the shelter through repeatable routines.\",\"Pristine ravine, west shelf cabin and nearby spruce terrain\",\"Night pressure, limited supplies and the absent frame make immediate return impossible.\",\"The efficient cabin footprint begins matching the ruin seen before entry.\",\"The protagonist gains a home and companion but also creates emotionally costly future evidence.\",\"Stranded survival becomes attachment in borrowed time.\",\"260\",\"Prioritise real taming, following, feeding, building and sit-release gameplay over montage abstraction.\"\n\"SQ-03\",\"3\",\"Prevent the remembered ruin and leave with Moss safely.\",\"Match construction details, stone-clad the timber route, pack for departure and neutralise the lava shelf before dawn.\",\"Cabin, ravine stair and lava shelf\",\"The portal may require obsidian, night makes immediate travel unsafe and one timber support remains hidden behind the cladding.\",\"Mining the cooled hazard opens a lateral lava source to the concealed support.\",\"The protagonist acts on foreknowledge, but the prevention operation becomes the fire’s physical cause.\",\"Hopeful control becomes urgent consequence.\",\"250\",\"Make every matched clue readable in block placement; the fire path remains provisional until tested.\"\n\"SQ-04\",\"4\",\"Reach and release Moss before the fire consumes the sitting place.\",\"Ignore storage and structural defence, route the single water source towards Moss and break the shortest wall path.\",\"Burning stair, cabin main room and bedside sitting place\",\"Fire has entered separated blocks, Moss remains seated and the protagonist begins below the cabin.\",\"He arrives within interaction range only after the terminal damage interval.\",\"Moss dies and the burned shell becomes the ruin from the opening.\",\"Immediate care becomes grief and full causal recognition.\",\"300\",\"The wolf must never appear penned; test the timing and stage the death without invented keepsakes or false mechanical claims.\"\n\"SQ-05\",\"5\",\"Reconstruct and activate the temporal frame before grief gives way to acceptance.\",\"Trace the burn, use mined obsidian, copy the remembered frame and improvise a lava-and-spruce ignition.\",\"Burned cabin route and north-wall portal cave\",\"The safe ignition tools are lost, the protagonist is damaged, rescue water removed a branch torch and a creeper can approach from darkness.\",\"The creeper kills him, but its blast releases lava to the remaining spruce and allows fire to activate the frame.\",\"The builder mystery, blast scar and active opening portal receive one tragic causal answer.\",\"Failed return becomes the threshold that began the journey.\",\"210\",\"No simultaneous double is needed; verify the blast, lava, fire, portal and item-destruction chain before lock.\""},{"path":".studio/internal/story/beat-sheet.csv","content":"beat_id,sequence_id,order,actor_id,attempt,interference,change,next_cause,purpose,reveal,visual,audio,estimated_seconds,dependency\n\"BEAT-01\",\"SQ-01\",\"1\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"Reach Moss through a burning cabin.\",\"Separated fire blocks cut across the direct route while Moss remains seated.\",\"He ignores storage, sends water towards the wolf and reaches forward before the film cuts.\",\"CE-07\",\"Open with danger, relationship priority and an honestly withheld outcome.\",\"The protagonist is already too late for an event whose cause is still unknown.\",\"Water passes a burning chest; Moss appears beyond flame for one instant.\",\"Fire, breaking blocks and one distressed wolf sound overtake a short narration line.\",\"35\",\"SC-01\"\n\"BEAT-02\",\"SQ-01\",\"2\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"Cross an unvisited ravine before night and understand the burned construction.\",\"A player-built stone shell and descending stair contradict the map edge and private-world history.\",\"He follows the surviving geometry towards purple light rather than leaving.\",\"CE-01\",\"Ground the title mystery in persistent environment rather than a person.\",\"The ruin has practical construction logic but no available builder.\",\"L-shaped foundation, granite notch, stone chest recess and missing timber align above the ravine.\",\"Wind and footsteps yield gradually to a portal hum.\",\"80\",\"SC-02\"\n\"BEAT-03\",\"SQ-01\",\"3\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"Inspect the active frame without committing to it.\",\"Blast damage, exposed lava and a plank remnant explain activation conditions but not authorship or destination.\",\"No ordinary explanation survives, so he chooses to enter.\",\"CE-02\",\"Honour the portal promise through protagonist agency.\",\"The frame is complete and active beside a recent physical event.\",\"Hand traces the cobblestone foot before crossing the portal plane.\",\"Portal ambience replaces restrained narration at the decision.\",\"65\",\"SC-03\"\n\"BEAT-04\",\"SQ-02\",\"4\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"Determine whether he reached another location or an earlier state.\",\"The portal vanishes behind him and every player block is absent.\",\"Matching natural landmarks prove the pristine ravine is the same place.\",\"CE-03\",\"Establish temporal displacement through geography.\",\"He stands before the construction he already saw destroyed.\",\"Bare north wall, intact granite seam and untouched west shelf repeat opening angles.\",\"Transition noise falls into ordinary cave and evening ambience.\",\"70\",\"SC-04\"\n\"BEAT-05\",\"SQ-02\",\"5\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"Survive the first night and deal with a nearby wolf after a skeleton fight.\",\"The skeleton creates danger before leaving only a small number of bones.\",\"The final taming attempt succeeds and Moss follows him to the shelter.\",\"CE-04\",\"Begin rapport through normal survival causality.\",\"The companion central to the cold open enters through earned gameplay.\",\"Skeleton drops bones; taming hearts appear; Moss crosses the threshold behind him.\",\"Combat gives way to the wolf’s ordinary panting and a restrained note of relief.\",\"90\",\"SC-05\"\n\"BEAT-06\",\"SQ-02\",\"6\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"Turn the shelter into a workable home while continuing ravine mining.\",\"Moss gets into build spaces, needs feeding after damage and cannot safely follow near lava.\",\"A repeated sit-before-mining and release-on-return routine becomes natural between them.\",\"CE-04\",\"Make the relationship lived, specific and mechanically consequential.\",\"The bedside sit state is a safety habit rather than confinement.\",\"Moss blocks a stair placement, follows on timber runs and waits beside the bed at dusk.\",\"Tool rhythm, door sounds and feeding replace heavy exposition.\",\"100\",\"SC-05\"\n\"BEAT-07\",\"SQ-03\",\"7\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"Finish the cabin foundation around the terrain.\",\"The granite notch, chest recess and stair rib reproduce details he saw in the ruin.\",\"A matched overlook confirms that he is building the burned cabin.\",\"CE-05\",\"Turn environmental familiarity into future recognition.\",\"The opening ruin belongs to the life he is making with Moss.\",\"Past and ruin compositions align on the L-shaped foundation.\",\"Construction sounds stop as remembered opening ambience returns briefly.\",\"80\",\"SC-06\"\n\"BEAT-08\",\"SQ-03\",\"8\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"Remove Moss and himself from the threatened future.\",\"Night travel is unsafe and the lava shelf beneath the timber route remains the clearest hazard.\",\"He plans departure at dawn, stone-clads the stair and chooses to neutralise the lava first.\",\"CE-05\",\"Give him a credible, active prevention strategy.\",\"He does not accept the ruin or wait passively for it.\",\"Packed supplies sit by the door while stone replaces exposed stair sections.\",\"Measured narration lays out the plan over real inventory and block actions.\",\"80\",\"SC-07\"\n\"BEAT-09\",\"SQ-03\",\"9\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"Cool the lava, collect return-frame material and keep Moss away from the mine.\",\"One stripped-spruce support remains continuous behind the new stone facing.\",\"He seats Moss beside the bed and mines the last visible obsidian block under running water.\",\"CE-06\",\"Join the relationship routine, prevention plan and fire geometry.\",\"The apparent firebreak is incomplete in a way the opening ruin fairly showed.\",\"Camera traces stone cladding while briefly revealing the concealed log seam.\",\"Water and pickaxe rhythm mask the first distant fire crackle.\",\"90\",\"SC-08\"\n\"BEAT-10\",\"SQ-04\",\"10\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"Contain the newly exposed side flow before it reaches the stair.\",\"Water cools the visible face while lava escapes laterally behind the cladding.\",\"The hidden support ignites and carries fire above him.\",\"CE-06\",\"Make prevention the ordinary physical cause of the disaster.\",\"No portal force or unseen person starts the fire.\",\"Lava disappears behind stone; fire appears along the upper timber route.\",\"Water hiss gives way to overhead crackle and Moss’s distant distress.\",\"90\",\"SC-08\"\n\"BEAT-11\",\"SQ-04\",\"11\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"Reach Moss without spending a second on the cabin or stored items.\",\"Separated fronts consume the shortest routes and Moss cannot cancel the sit command.\",\"He completes the cold-open action but reaches interaction range after the final damage interval.\",\"CE-07\",\"Deliver the relationship tragedy without a false property-versus-life choice.\",\"Moss dies in the same fire whose ruin appeared at the beginning.\",\"Water passes storage; a wall is broken; the empty sitting place remains after flame clears.\",\"Narration falls silent through the attempt and returns only after the fire sound recedes.\",\"150\",\"SC-09\"\n\"BEAT-12\",\"SQ-04\",\"12\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"Trace the fire backwards and determine whether any action remains.\",\"The burn path proves that his hidden support and mining operation caused the known ruin.\",\"He gathers the mined obsidian and decides the remembered portal may offer one more chance.\",\"CE-08\",\"Convert grief and recognition into the final active objective.\",\"The opening ruin, current fire and portal materials belong to one causal history.\",\"Missing timber leads from the bedside room to the lava shelf and stacked obsidian.\",\"Sparse narration sits over block inspection rather than an explanatory speech.\",\"60\",\"SC-10\"\n\"BEAT-13\",\"SQ-05\",\"13\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"Build the exact remembered frame before stopping to rest or resupply.\",\"The cabin fire has destroyed flint and steel, spare iron, shield and much of his food.\",\"He reproduces the cobblestone foot and asymmetry, then prepares a lava-and-spruce ignition.\",\"CE-08\",\"Pay off the builder mystery through visible work.\",\"He is the portal’s physical builder, using materials with ordinary origins.\",\"Opening close-ups recur as each block is placed from the causal side.\",\"Mining and placement sounds echo the opening inspection in reverse order.\",\"90\",\"SC-11\"\n\"BEAT-14\",\"SQ-05\",\"14\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"Wait for fire to form inside the frame and take him back.\",\"Rescue water removed a torch, the lateral branch is dark and a creeper reaches close range while he watches the plank.\",\"The creeper kills him and breaks the lip retaining the ignition lava.\",\"CE-09\",\"Make the final reversal an earned interaction of grief, darkness and hostile gameplay.\",\"The opening blast cavity and absent builder come from this death.\",\"A hiss turns him away from the unlit frame one beat too late.\",\"Portal silence, creeper hiss and explosion replace narration.\",\"80\",\"SC-11\"\n\"BEAT-15\",\"SQ-05\",\"15\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"Complete the ignition through the arrangement chosen before death.\",\"The blast removes part of the cradle but also opens lava to the remaining spruce.\",\"Fire forms inside the frame, the portal activates and the image matches the earlier entry.\",\"CE-10\",\"Close builder, activation and journey questions in one visual pay-off.\",\"The failed return attempt created the portal that began it.\",\"Drops burn, purple fills the frame and a match cut returns to the entering hand.\",\"Fire catches, portal ambience rises and the proposed final line lands after the visual proof.\",\"40\",\"SC-12\""},{"path":".studio/internal/story/scene-cards.csv","content":"scene_id,sequence_id,location,time,pov,scene_purpose,entry_state,objective,reason_objective_matters,obstacle,opposing_agent,tactics,information_revealed,information_withheld,emotional_progression,visual_event,turn,outcome,exit_state,new_question,setup_or_payoff,story_value,production_requirements\n\"SC-01\",\"SQ-01\",\"Burning west-rim cabin and shared sleeping area\",\"World W3 at night; personal order 5; presented first as a cold open\",\"Close protagonist\",\"Promise immediate danger, establish that Moss outranks the structure and withhold only the rescue outcome.\",\"The cabin is already burning and the protagonist is below Moss’s sitting place.\",\"Reach and release Moss.\",\"The relationship is the human stake and the opening must not imply a choice between life and property.\",\"Separated fire blocks cut the route while the wolf remains seated.\",\"Fire geometry and elapsed time.\",\"Ignore storage, empty water towards Moss and break the shortest wall route.\",\"The protagonist spends no action defending possessions once Moss is threatened.\",\"The fire’s cause, portal connection and Moss’s fate remain unknown.\",\"Panic to concentrated care, then interruption.\",\"Water passes a burning chest as Moss appears through flame.\",\"His hand reaches interaction distance and the film cuts.\",\"The rescue remains unresolved but morally unambiguous.\",\"What caused the fire, and did he reach Moss in time?\",\"SP-05;SP-07\",\"Hook, promise and emotional orientation.\",\"Short reproducible fire stage, seated wolf line of sight, real water and breaking actions, and no claim that the outcome has been production-tested.\"\n\"SC-02\",\"SQ-01\",\"Ruined west-rim cabin and ravine overlook\",\"World W5 near dusk; personal order 1\",\"Close protagonist\",\"Introduce the environmental authorship mystery without suggesting a visible intruder.\",\"The protagonist is on a solo expedition at the unexplored edge of his map.\",\"Cross the ravine before night and understand whether the ruin offers shelter.\",\"Its construction should not exist in a region he has never visited.\",\"The wooden upper cabin is gone, but player-made stone geometry and a descending stair remain.\",\"Persistent aftermath, fading light and the protagonist’s own knowledge of the save.\",\"Check map context, read the foundation, inspect the protected chest recess and follow the stair.\",\"The ruin was built efficiently around the same natural granite seam and stone rib.\",\"It is his later work, Moss lived here and the portal waits below.\",\"Ordinary caution to unsettled recognition without an answer.\",\"A repeated overlook frames the L-shaped foundation and purple light under the ravine lip.\",\"The portal hum supplies a live route beneath a dead structure.\",\"He descends rather than camping in the ruin.\",\"Why is an active portal connected to a cabin with no history?\",\"SP-01;SP-06\",\"Grounds mystery in Minecraft construction and spatial persistence.\",\"Burned cabin state, map-edge or coordinate context, reusable overlook, no body, no sign and no silhouette.\"\n\"SC-03\",\"SQ-01\",\"North-wall portal cave\",\"World W5 at dusk; personal order 1\",\"Close protagonist\",\"Pose the title question through block evidence and make entry an accountable decision.\",\"He expects the stair to lead to the ruin’s mine or cellar.\",\"Explain the active frame before choosing whether to cross.\",\"The portal is the only surviving system that may account for the impossible construction.\",\"A blast cavity, exposed lava, cobblestone foot and plank remnant show recent causality but no builder.\",\"The active frame and uncertain destination.\",\"Inspect block faces, lava access, dark branch, frame economy and absence of recoverable drops.\",\"The portal was physically constructed and activated near a recent explosion.\",\"The protagonist built it after Moss’s death; fire rather than the explosion directly activated it.\",\"Unease to chosen risk.\",\"His hand rests on the water-cooled foot before moving into the portal plane.\",\"He enters when observation alone cannot answer the contradiction.\",\"The known world gives way to temporal displacement.\",\"Where and when does this portal lead?\",\"SP-02;SP-03;SP-09;SP-10;SP-11;SP-12\",\"Inciting agency and a dense but fair clue cluster.\",\"Exact portal hero set, blast cavity, lava and plank continuity, controlled transition, and no unsupported explosion-to-fire implication.\"\n\"SC-04\",\"SQ-02\",\"Bare north-wall cave and pristine ravine\",\"World W0 several days before W5; personal order 2\",\"Close protagonist\",\"Prove earlier time through missing construction and repeated natural geography.\",\"He expects a Nether arrival or another built exit.\",\"Determine location, time state and immediate survival options.\",\"Without orientation he cannot choose whether to wait, travel or reconstruct a route.\",\"The portal plane collapses to bare stone, evening approaches and the distant established base is unreachable before night.\",\"Temporal displacement, terrain and daylight.\",\"Match the granite seam, stone rib, lava shelf and tree line; then secure the only flat ledge.\",\"This is the same ravine before every player-placed block from the opening.\",\"The exact interval, portal builder and future relationship remain unknown.\",\"Disorientation to practical resolve.\",\"The opening portal outline becomes bare rock in the same camera position.\",\"He chooses a temporary camp beside the evidence instead of a blind night journey.\",\"Stranded investigation becomes survival.\",\"What will he build in the place whose ruin he already saw?\",\"PAY-02;SP-01\",\"Establishes the temporal rule through environment.\",\"Pristine saved state, matched camera positions, day-night pressure and no second-avatar capture.\"\n\"SC-05\",\"SQ-02\",\"West-rim shelter, spruce terrain and cabin interior\",\"World W0 through W1 across several in-game days; personal order 3\",\"Close protagonist and Moss\",\"Create an earned relationship and turn a temporary camp into the future home.\",\"The protagonist is alone, provisionally sheltered and still seeking a return route.\",\"Survive, tame Moss and establish a workable daily routine.\",\"The later loss needs lived attachment, and every future structure needs an ordinary origin.\",\"A skeleton, limited bones, building work, routine damage and the lava hazard complicate progress.\",\"Night mobs, resource scarcity and ordinary animal behaviour.\",\"Defeat the skeleton, attempt taming, feed Moss, gather spruce, build around the wolf and use sit only before ravine mining.\",\"Moss enters the story through normal play; the bedside wait and release-on-return pattern becomes habitual.\",\"The routine will make self-evacuation impossible during the fire.\",\"Wariness to humour, trust and ease.\",\"Moss occupies a planned stair block, follows a timber run and later waits beside the bed until released.\",\"The protagonist stops calling the cabin temporary.\",\"A stranded interval becomes a shared home.\",\"Why do parts of this new home feel familiar?\",\"SP-04;PAY-01\",\"Relationship, normality, geography and mechanical setup.\",\"Real skeleton drop, taming attempts, feeding, follow and sit states, staged construction progress and no pen or doghouse.\"\n\"SC-06\",\"SQ-03\",\"Cabin foundation, stone chest recess and ravine overlook\",\"World W2 in daylight; personal order 4\",\"Close protagonist\",\"Convert repeated environmental detail into recognition of the cabin’s future.\",\"He believes resemblance to the opening ruin may be incidental.\",\"Compare the finished build against remembered landmarks.\",\"If the ruin is this home, Moss is already inside a known danger pattern.\",\"Fire removed the opening cabin’s upper form, so proof depends on surviving stone geometry rather than exact decoration.\",\"Persistent blocks and the protagonist’s reluctance to accept the implication.\",\"Align the granite notch, L-shaped footing, chest recess and stair rib from the opening viewpoint.\",\"He has built the cabin whose burned remains preceded the portal.\",\"The exact fire source, Moss’s fate and whether departure can avoid it remain unknown.\",\"Unease to dread, then active determination.\",\"A matched camera move turns the intact foundation into the remembered ruin without showing another person.\",\"He packs to leave with Moss at dawn and identifies lava as the most credible hazard.\",\"Mystery becomes a prevention problem.\",\"Can practical changes stop the remembered fire?\",\"PAY-01;SP-06\",\"Major recontextualisation through construction rather than exposition.\",\"Coordinate-accurate foundation, granite, recess and stair matches with reversible intact and ruined states.\"\n\"SC-07\",\"SQ-03\",\"Cabin, ravine stair and lava-shelf approach\",\"World W2 at dusk; personal order 4\",\"Close protagonist and Moss\",\"Give the protagonist a sensible prevention strategy and prepare the hidden flaw.\",\"He knows the cabin burns and intends to leave with Moss.\",\"Make the cabin safe for one final night, neutralise the lava and depart at dawn.\",\"Immediate night travel is dangerous, but waiting without changing the fire route would be passive.\",\"One stripped-spruce support remains behind the new stone cladding, and the return mechanism may require obsidian.\",\"Terrain, night pressure and incomplete visual access.\",\"Pack supplies, replace exposed timber with stone, keep one water source ready and inspect the lava shelf.\",\"He prioritises departure with Moss and does not plan to defend the cabin as property.\",\"The cladding conceals a continuous flammable route and mining will expose lateral lava.\",\"Fear to practical hope.\",\"Packed bundles sit beside the door while stone covers the visible stair and one log seam disappears behind it.\",\"He seats Moss beside the bed and descends for the final operation.\",\"Is the apparent firebreak actually complete?\",\"SP-04;SP-06;SP-07\",\"Agency, credible risk management and causal preparation.\",\"Readable shared sleeping area, packed-departure props, stone-cladding build, concealed-support cross-section and complete production blocking.\"\n\"SC-08\",\"SQ-03\",\"Lava shelf and stone-clad stair\",\"World W2 moving into W3 at night; personal order 4\",\"Close protagonist\",\"Turn prevention into the ordinary fire cause and source the portal materials.\",\"Moss is safely seated above, and the visible lava is covered by flowing water.\",\"Neutralise the shelf and collect enough obsidian for a possible controlled frame.\",\"Removing the hazard appears to protect Moss and preserve the only observed return technology.\",\"A second lava source lies behind the final cooled block and can flow laterally beyond the visible water.\",\"Fluid geometry, hidden timber and the protagonist’s dual objective.\",\"Keep water on the exposed face, mine the obsidian and inspect the opening beneath it.\",\"The cobblestone foot and obsidian used later have local origins; a side flow reaches the concealed support.\",\"The protagonist does not yet know fire has already climbed behind him.\",\"Concentration to alarm.\",\"Water hisses on the visible face while orange light disappears behind stone and reappears on timber above.\",\"Moss’s distress reveals that the fire has outrun his viewpoint.\",\"Prevention becomes rescue emergency.\",\"Can he reach the seated wolf before fire reaches the room?\",\"PAY-03;PAY-06;SP-08\",\"Physical causality, escalation and material provenance.\",\"Target-version test for water direction, lava source placement, ignition range, fire tick timing and hidden-support visibility.\"\n\"SC-09\",\"SQ-04\",\"Burning cabin, shortest wall route and bedside sitting place\",\"World W3 at night; personal order 5\",\"Close protagonist and Moss\",\"Complete the cold open, resolve Moss’s fate and avoid a false house-versus-wolf dilemma.\",\"The protagonist hears Moss while the fire moves through separated cabin blocks.\",\"Reach and release Moss immediately.\",\"The wolf is the only objective once danger is known.\",\"The protagonist begins below the cabin, one water source cannot suppress every fire block and Moss remains seated.\",\"Fire timing and trained animal state.\",\"Ignore storage, place water towards the room, break the shortest wall and attempt the release interaction.\",\"The sit routine prevents autonomous escape and the ruin is created by the same fire already observed.\",\"The later portal decision and fatal ignition remain unknown.\",\"Urgency to hope at proximity, then grief.\",\"The cold-open reach continues one beat further; flame clears to the empty sitting position after death.\",\"The interaction comes after the terminal damage interval.\",\"Moss is dead and the cabin is becoming the opening ruin.\",\"Will the protagonist accept the loss or use the remembered portal as another attempt?\",\"PAY-04;PAY-05;PAY-07\",\"Emotional climax and relationship consequence.\",\"Mechanically credible wolf position, visible prioritisation, controlled damage timing, no invented dropped collar and no claim of completed testing.\"\n\"SC-10\",\"SQ-04\",\"Burned cabin route, lava shelf and stored obsidian\",\"World W3 late night; personal order 5\",\"Close protagonist\",\"Trace the disaster, connect it to the opening ruin and generate the final objective from grief.\",\"Moss is dead, the cabin cannot be recovered and the protagonist has survived with damaged equipment.\",\"Find the fire’s cause and any remaining route to a time before it.\",\"Causal knowledge is the only thing he can still act upon.\",\"The missing-block path implicates his own support and mining, while the standing portal from memory does not yet exist.\",\"Persistent aftermath and grief-driven reasoning.\",\"Follow the burn line, compare the surviving stone shell and gather the obsidian already mined.\",\"The current ruin is the opening ruin, and he possesses the materials needed to become the portal builder.\",\"The creeper, death and activation geometry remain unknown.\",\"Numbness to desperate purpose.\",\"The camera follows absent timber from Moss’s room to the lava shelf, then lands on ten obsidian blocks.\",\"He carries the obsidian to the bare portal outline.\",\"Grief becomes immediate reconstruction.\",\"Can the remembered frame take him back before the fire?\",\"SP-02;SP-08\",\"Causal recognition and launch of the final attempt.\",\"Burned reset matching SC-02, legible missing-block route, ten sourced obsidian blocks and subdued gameplay pacing.\"\n\"SC-11\",\"SQ-05\",\"North-wall portal cave and dark lateral branch\",\"World W4 before dawn; personal order 6\",\"Close protagonist\",\"Reveal the builder through action and make haste create lethal exposure.\",\"He has obsidian, one water bucket, local spruce, reduced health and no safe ignition tool.\",\"Build and activate the remembered portal immediately.\",\"He believes another crossing is the only remaining chance to reach Moss alive.\",\"The frame is inert, flint and spare iron were lost, rescue water removed a branch torch and a creeper can approach unseen.\",\"The grief-driven protagonist and the creeper’s ordinary hostile behaviour.\",\"Copy the economical frame, recreate the foot, place a lava source and spruce cradle, then watch for fire.\",\"He physically built every opening portal detail after Moss’s death.\",\"The blast will enable rather than directly create the eventual fire inside the frame.\",\"Purpose to fragile hope, then fatal shock.\",\"The opening close-ups repeat in reverse until a hiss turns him from the still-dark frame.\",\"The creeper detonates at close range and kills him.\",\"The builder is absent; the retaining lip is broken and the ignition geometry has changed.\",\"Will the blast extinguish the attempt or complete it?\",\"PAY-02;PAY-08;PAY-09;PAY-10\",\"Builder confirmation, terminal reversal and blast-scar origin.\",\"Exact block reconstruction, washed-torch continuity, controlled creeper approach, credible health and armour state, Hardcore death language and no simultaneous avatar.\"\n\"SC-12\",\"SQ-05\",\"North-wall portal cave\",\"World W4 immediately after death leading to W5 discovery; personal order closes\",\"Objective observer followed by the opening protagonist’s hand\",\"Resolve activation, absence and loop closure through one continuous environmental event.\",\"The protagonist is dead, the frame is intact and altered lava is reaching the surviving spruce.\",\"Complete the causal consequence of the ignition arrangement and reconnect it to the opening entry.\",\"Without activation, the portal discovery and entire personal journey cannot occur.\",\"Part of the cradle is gone, drops are exposed to lava or fire and flame must form inside the valid frame.\",\"Fluid and fire behaviour established by the protagonist’s prior placement.\",\"Hold on the remaining plank, follow fire into the frame and match cut from activation to the earlier entry.\",\"Ordinary fire, not the explosion itself, activates the portal; the opening frame is his failed return attempt.\",\"The external first origin of temporal routing remains intentionally open.\",\"Shock to tragic recognition and reflective residue.\",\"Drops burn, the portal turns purple and the composition becomes the opening hand crossing.\",\"The physical effect shown first receives its final cause.\",\"The protagonist’s worldline closes with no surviving builder or rescued wolf.\",\"Where did the temporal routing pattern first originate?\",\"PAY-10;PAY-11;OPEN-01\",\"Mystery closure, causal closure and final visual transformation.\",\"Version-tested blast-to-lava-to-fire-to-portal chain, controllable item destruction, portal persistence and frame-accurate match cut.\""},{"path":".studio/internal/story/information-map.csv","content":"info_id,question_or_fact,audience_knows_before,audience_expects_before,character_knowledge_before,delivery_beat_or_scene,audience_knows_after,audience_expects_after,emotional_orientation_after,withheld_or_revealed,fairness_setup_ids,payoff_or_resolution_id,notes\n\"INF-01\",\"Who built the portal?\",\"The frame stands in an unvisited private-world region beside a player-built ruin and no visible person.\",\"Its builder may be connected to the burned cabin, but environmental damage does not identify anyone.\",\"The protagonist knows only that he did not build it before his opening discovery.\",\"SC-11\",\"The protagonist constructs the exact frame after Moss dies, using locally mined materials.\",\"The remaining question is how his failed ignition produced the active opening state.\",\"Mystery satisfaction mixed with dread about his haste.\",\"revealed\",\"SP-02|SP-03|SP-08\",\"PAY-02\",\"No silhouette, message, voice or second-player hypothesis carries the reveal.\"\n\"INF-02\",\"Where and when does the first crossing lead?\",\"The opening ruin and portal occupy a recognisable ravine.\",\"The destination may be the Nether, another world state or the same place at another time.\",\"The protagonist has no tested model for the portal.\",\"SC-04\",\"The same natural ravine exists before every player-placed block, establishing an earlier state.\",\"The audience expects the protagonist’s actions to create at least some opening details.\",\"Disorientation becomes anticipatory recognition.\",\"revealed\",\"SP-01|SP-12\",\"PAY-01\",\"Natural geography provides proof before narration interprets it.\"\n\"INF-03\",\"Whose burned cabin appeared in the opening?\",\"The ruin has an L-shaped foundation, granite notch, chest recess and stair rib.\",\"It may belong to the unknown portal builder.\",\"The protagonist remembers the layout but initially treats similarities as incidental.\",\"SC-06\",\"His practical terrain-driven construction matches every surviving feature.\",\"The cabin is expected to burn unless his prevention changes the causal path.\",\"Attachment turns the opening ruin into a personal threat.\",\"revealed\",\"SP-01\",\"PAY-01\",\"Recognition occurs after the relationship and cabin have acquired value.\"\n\"INF-04\",\"Why can Moss not leave the fire alone?\",\"Moss repeatedly waits beside the bed when the protagonist uses the sit command before ravine work.\",\"The routine appears to keep the wolf away from ordinary mining danger.\",\"The protagonist trusts the cabin and firebreak for one final night.\",\"SC-09\",\"The sitting state persists during the fire and requires the protagonist to reach and release Moss.\",\"The outcome depends on route timing rather than a pen, locked doghouse or human-like indecision.\",\"Dread grounded in familiar gameplay behaviour.\",\"revealed\",\"SP-04\",\"PAY-04\",\"Moss is not enclosed; the command state is the constraint.\"\n\"INF-05\",\"Can foreknowledge prevent the fire and save Moss?\",\"The protagonist has recognised the ruin early enough to plan departure and remove the apparent hazard.\",\"A stone firebreak and immediate action may produce a different outcome.\",\"He believes the visible lava route can be neutralised before dawn.\",\"SC-09;SC-12\",\"His prevention exposes the hidden path, Moss dies, and the later portal attempt creates the opening threshold.\",\"The audience expects no branch, reset or successful retry after his death.\",\"Tragic recognition without moral contempt.\",\"revealed\",\"SP-05|SP-06|SP-07\",\"PAY-05\",\"No invisible correction occurs; each result follows chosen actions and physical conditions.\"\n\"INF-06\",\"What starts the cabin fire?\",\"The lava shelf, stone-clad stair and concealed log seam have been shown before ignition.\",\"Lava remains the likely hazard, but the firebreak appears to separate it from the cabin.\",\"The protagonist believes water covers the visible lava face.\",\"SC-08;SC-09\",\"Mining the final cooled block opens a lateral source that reaches the continuous support behind the cladding.\",\"The audience can trace the missing-block route into the cabin.\",\"Causal dread and frustration at an understandable mistake.\",\"revealed\",\"SP-06\",\"PAY-06\",\"Exact range and timing remain subject to version-specific testing.\"\n\"INF-07\",\"Does Moss survive the cold-open fire?\",\"The cold open shows an immediate rescue attempt but cuts before interaction.\",\"The protagonist may reach Moss because he ignores the structure and acts directly.\",\"He knows only that Moss is seated above when the fire begins.\",\"SC-09\",\"He reaches the sitting place after the terminal damage interval and Moss dies without an invented dropped token.\",\"The portal construction is expected to arise from grief rather than a building-loss dilemma.\",\"Grief and unfair-seeming lateness grounded in prior mechanics.\",\"revealed\",\"SP-04|SP-05|SP-07\",\"PAY-05\",\"The narration never claims survival or death before the event is shown.\"\n\"INF-08\",\"How was the opening portal lit?\",\"The frame is active beside exposed lava, blast damage and one remaining plank.\",\"The blast may appear to have activated it directly.\",\"The protagonist later knows only that he must reproduce an active frame.\",\"SC-12\",\"The explosion breaks the retaining lip; lava then ignites remaining spruce and ordinary fire forms inside the frame.\",\"The audience can distinguish geometry change from fire generation.\",\"Mechanical satisfaction inside the tragic reveal.\",\"revealed\",\"SP-03|SP-11\",\"PAY-11\",\"Testing must confirm the exact arrangement; no false claim that creeper blasts create fire is made.\"\n\"INF-09\",\"What caused the blast cavity and absence of a builder?\",\"The opening cave contains recent missing blocks, exposed lava and no body or recoverable inventory.\",\"The builder may have left or died in the environmental event.\",\"The protagonist has not yet imagined himself as the builder.\",\"SC-11;SC-12\",\"A creeper kills him, creates the cavity and leaves his drops to be destroyed by lava or fire.\",\"The later opening self can arrive without seeing another avatar or implausible remains.\",\"Shock followed by complete recontextualisation.\",\"revealed\",\"SP-09|SP-10\",\"PAY-10\",\"Item destruction and portal persistence require capture testing.\"\n\"INF-10\",\"Where did the portal’s temporal routing first originate?\",\"The frame has ordinary materials but its route does not match vanilla portal behaviour.\",\"Builder identity may appear likely to explain the temporal property as well.\",\"The protagonist copies the arrangement empirically and never learns its external first design.\",\"SC-12\",\"He is confirmed as builder and activator, but no external origin for the copied routing information is supplied.\",\"The question remains reflective rather than a missing cause for the fire, death or journey.\",\"Open-ended unease after practical closure.\",\"withheld\",\"SP-12\",\"OPEN-01\",\"The intentionally open resolution remains a proposal requiring human approval before canon lock.\""},{"path":".studio/internal/story/setup-payoff-ledger.csv","content":"setup_id,setup_beat_or_scene,setup,initial_audience_interpretation,payoff_id,payoff_beat_or_scene,payoff,planned_payoff_type,resolution_status,intentionally_open_reason,latest_acceptable_point,notes\n\"SP-01\",\"SC-02;SC-04\",\"The burned L-shaped foundation, granite notch, chest recess and pristine west shelf share exact geography.\",\"The ruin belonged to whoever made the portal, while the earlier shelf offers a new start.\",\"PAY-01\",\"SC-05;SC-06\",\"Terrain and efficient survival choices lead the protagonist to build the same cabin whose remains he saw.\",\"reinterpretation\",\"planned\",\"\",\"SC-06\",\"Use matched coordinates and lenses; do not rely on narration alone.\"\n\"SP-02\",\"SC-03\",\"A complete active frame stands in a private region the protagonist has not previously visited.\",\"The portal has an absent but unknown builder.\",\"PAY-02\",\"SC-10;SC-11\",\"After Moss dies, the protagonist mines and places every block of the exact frame.\",\"confirmation\",\"planned\",\"\",\"SC-11\",\"Builder identity must resolve through construction before the activation reversal.\"\n\"SP-03\",\"SC-03\",\"The frame has an asymmetrical cobblestone foot, exposed lava and a surviving spruce remnant.\",\"They are rough repair, blast debris or accidental activation evidence.\",\"PAY-03\",\"SC-08;SC-11;SC-12\",\"Water cooling creates the foot, the protagonist places the ignition cradle, and the blast releases lava to its surviving plank.\",\"reinterpretation\",\"planned\",\"\",\"SC-12\",\"Every block requires an ordinary material origin and stable final coordinate.\"\n\"SP-04\",\"SC-05\",\"Moss routinely sits beside the bed only while the protagonist works near ravine lava.\",\"It is a sensible command that keeps the wolf out of mining danger.\",\"PAY-04\",\"SC-07;SC-09\",\"The same routine leaves Moss unable to self-evacuate while the protagonist begins below the burning cabin.\",\"tragic_fulfilment\",\"planned\",\"\",\"SC-09\",\"No pen, doghouse, locked room or autonomous rule change may replace the sit mechanic.\"\n\"SP-05\",\"SC-01\",\"The cold open cuts as the protagonist reaches through fire towards Moss.\",\"The rescue may still succeed because he clearly prioritises the wolf.\",\"PAY-05\",\"SC-09\",\"The completed action reaches Moss after the terminal fire-damage interval.\",\"tragic_fulfilment\",\"planned\",\"\",\"SC-09\",\"The cut withholds outcome honestly; narration cannot assert survival or death early.\"\n\"SP-06\",\"SC-02;SC-07\",\"A stone-clad interruption in the burned stair appears to have been a firebreak, while a stripped-log seam passes behind it in the intact build.\",\"The stone should prevent lava fire from reaching the cabin.\",\"PAY-06\",\"SC-08;SC-09\",\"Lateral lava reaches the concealed continuous support and bypasses the visible cladding.\",\"tragic_fulfilment\",\"planned\",\"\",\"SC-09\",\"Mechanical geometry and visual legibility need target-version testing.\"\n\"SP-07\",\"SC-01;SC-07\",\"The protagonist carries one movable water source and packs to leave rather than defend the cabin.\",\"Water may be sufficient because he has planned for the known hazard.\",\"PAY-07\",\"SC-09\",\"He spends the water solely on reaching Moss, but separated fire and distance consume the available time.\",\"character_choice\",\"planned\",\"\",\"SC-09\",\"The limitation is coverage and timing, not an invented ban on collecting water.\"\n\"SP-08\",\"SC-08\",\"Cooling the shelf produces local obsidian while the future portal frame remains absent.\",\"The blocks may offer a controlled route home after the hazard is removed.\",\"PAY-08\",\"SC-10;SC-11\",\"He carries the same mined obsidian into the cave and builds the remembered economical frame.\",\"confirmation\",\"planned\",\"\",\"SC-11\",\"No obsidian or other physical material is bootstrapped.\"\n\"SP-09\",\"SC-03;SC-11\",\"The opening portal cave has a dark lateral branch and the later rescue water washes its torch away.\",\"The darkness is minor environmental continuity.\",\"PAY-09\",\"SC-11\",\"A creeper approaches through that branch while the protagonist watches the ignition cradle.\",\"escalation\",\"planned\",\"\",\"SC-11\",\"Confirm approach and light-level logic; do not assert an untested spawn beside the player.\"\n\"SP-10\",\"SC-03\",\"No body or meaningful dropped token remains beside the opening blast cavity.\",\"The builder may have escaped or left no useful evidence.\",\"PAY-10\",\"SC-11;SC-12\",\"The creeper kills the protagonist and ordinary drops are consumed by nearby lava or fire before the later discovery.\",\"reinterpretation\",\"planned\",\"\",\"SC-12\",\"Do not invent a wolf collar drop, corpse object or non-vanilla clue.\"\n\"SP-11\",\"SC-03\",\"The opening frame is active despite blast damage and no surviving flint-and-steel evidence.\",\"The explosion may seem to have lit it directly.\",\"PAY-11\",\"SC-12\",\"The blast breaks the retaining lip; lava ignites remaining spruce and a later fire block activates the frame.\",\"confirmation\",\"planned\",\"\",\"SC-12\",\"Explicitly preserve the distinction between explosion, geometry change, fire spread and portal activation.\"\n\"SP-12\",\"SC-03;SC-04\",\"An ordinary-looking frame routes the protagonist to the same ravine before the frame exists.\",\"The builder reveal may also explain where the temporal design originated.\",\"OPEN-01\",\"SC-12\",\"The protagonist is the physical builder and activator, but the first external origin of the copied temporal routing remains unanswered.\",\"reinterpretation\",\"intentionally_open\",\"The information loop supports the authorship mystery and is not needed to explain materials, fire, death or activation.\",\"SC-12\",\"Open status remains unlocked pending human approval.\""},{"path":".studio/internal/story/relationships.csv","content":"relationship_id,character_a,character_b,public_relationship,private_tension,shared_history,what_a_wants_from_b,what_b_wants_from_a,power_balance,secret,predicted_change\n\"REL-01\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"WOLF\",\"A stranded solo player and his tamed wolf build and inhabit a compact ravine cabin together.\",\"The protagonist’s sit-before-mining routine is genuine care in ordinary conditions, but Moss’s dependable obedience becomes lethal when fire reaches the shared room.\",\"The protagonist earns bones from a skeleton encounter, tames Moss, feeds it after damage, gathers materials with it, builds around its interruptions and releases it whenever he returns from the ravine.\",\"He wants Moss near him during ordinary life, safely waiting during lava work and ultimately travelling away from the remembered ruin with him.\",\"Expressed behaviourally, Moss wants proximity, food when hurt and a clear follow command after waiting.\",\"The protagonist controls sitting, following and route choice; during the fire, the need to reach Moss controls every action he takes.\",\"The burned ruin seen before their relationship is the future remains of their home, and the portal beside it is the protagonist’s later grief-driven construction.\",\"Tentative taming becomes comfortable shared routine, then irreversible loss that motivates the portal’s creation.\""},{"path":".studio/internal/story/continuity-bible.md","content":"# Continuity bible — The Portal That Nobody Made\n\n## Canon authority\n\n- Canon owner: Luan\n- Current canon version: Story proposal 0.2 — unlocked, 16 July 2026\n- Locked upstream artefacts: None stated. The supplied Direction is explicitly unlocked.\n- Rule for proposed canon changes: record the proposal, dependency impact and human decision before altering locked work.\n- Status of this bible: proposed continuity for human review; it does not record a Story lock, audience result or production validation.\n- Major proposed changes: tragic wolf death, terminal protagonist death, Hardcore mode, no visible second self, no wolf enclosure, and a new worldline in which the opening ruin predates the protagonist’s personal arrival.\n\n## Timeline\n\n### World chronology\n\n| World time | Objective event |\n|---|---|\n| W0 — earlier ravine arrival | The travelling protagonist is ejected through a temporary temporal plane into the pristine north-wall cave. No portal frame or cabin exists. His pre-travel self is several days away on a distant solo expedition and never enters this geography during W0–W4. |\n| W1 — homestead period | The travelling protagonist camps on the west shelf, obtains bones from a skeleton, tames Moss and builds the spruce-and-cobblestone cabin, protected chest recess and ravine stair. The normal routine is to let Moss follow during safe work, feed it after damage, sit it beside the bed before lava work and release it on return. |\n| W2 — recognition and prevention | The protagonist matches his foundation, granite notch, chest recess and stair rib to the burned ruin he remembers. He packs to leave with Moss at dawn, stone-clads the visible timber route and begins cooling the lava shelf. One stripped-spruce support remains continuous behind the cladding. |\n| W3 — fire and loss | Mining the final visible cooled block opens a lateral lava flow. It reaches the concealed support and fire travels into the cabin. Moss is still sitting beside the bed. The protagonist directs every rescue action towards the wolf but reaches the interaction after terminal fire damage. Moss dies and the cabin burns to its opening state. |\n| W4 — portal construction and death | The protagonist traces the fire, uses the locally mined obsidian to build the exact remembered frame and prepares a lava-and-spruce ignition. Rescue water has removed a torch from a lateral branch. A creeper approaches and kills him. The blast breaks the lava-retaining lip; subsequent lava ignition and fire activate the portal. Ordinary drops are destroyed by lava or fire. |\n| W5 — discovery | The pre-travel protagonist reaches the ravine later in world chronology. He finds the burned cabin, blast cavity and active portal, sees no other self, and enters. The portal sends him to W0, where he becomes the travelling protagonist described above. |\n\n### Protagonist’s personal order\n\nP1 W5 burned-ravine discovery → P2 W5 portal entry → P3 W0 pristine ejection → P4 W1 cabin and Moss relationship → P5 W2 recognition and prevention → P6 W3 fire and Moss’s death → P7 W4 portal construction, creeper death and activation.\n\nThe film presents a short fragment from P6 before P1, then follows P1–P7. The fragment withholds Moss’s fate through a cut but does not present a different event.\n\n### Version overlap\n\nDuring W0–W4, the protagonist’s pre-travel version exists elsewhere in the same world on the distant route that will eventually reach the ravine at W5. The versions do not share a scene, line of sight, sound cue, item exchange or direct intervention. No double-avatar image is required.\n\n### Closed causal information\n\nThe protagonist remembers the frame and later copies it. Obsidian, cobblestone, spruce, water, bones and equipment have ordinary origins. The physical matter is not bootstrapped. The first external origin of the frame arrangement’s temporal routing remains intentionally open in this proposal. It cannot be expanded into a supernatural agent or paradox-correction force without human approval.\n\n## Character states and knowledge\n\n| Scene | Protagonist state, knowledge and objective | Possessions and ability | Moss state |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| SC-01 | Presented out of order inside the fire; knows Moss is sitting above and spends no action on the cabin. | One water bucket, damaged ordinary equipment and tools capable of breaking the shortest wall. | Alive at entry, seated beside the bed and taking fire damage; outcome withheld by the cut. |\n| SC-02 | On a solo expedition; knows the map edge is unvisited and the ruin is impossible but not who made it. | Ordinary expedition kit, map or coordinate context, food, tools, one water bucket and serviceable defence equipment. | Not yet met in personal order; already dead in W5 world chronology. |\n| SC-03 | Knows the frame is deliberate and recently affected by a blast; chooses entry. | Same expedition kit; no portal materials acquired. | Absent from viewpoint and never represented by remains or an invented token. |\n| SC-04 | Knows he is in the same ravine before construction and is stranded for the night. | Gear carried through temporal transit; no standing return frame. | A wild wolf exists nearby but has not been tamed. |\n| SC-05 | Builds a life while studying the portal site and gradually treats the cabin as home. | Local bones, spruce, cobblestone, food and routine tools; water bucket retained. | Tamed, fed after damage, following during safe work and sitting only before dangerous ravine work. |\n| SC-06 | Recognises that his foundation is the opening ruin; decides to prevent the fire and leave with Moss. | Packed supplies, tools and one water source; no completed frame. | Alive, healthy enough to travel and normally following. |\n| SC-07 | Believes stone cladding and lava neutralisation will make one final night safe. | Cobblestone fireproofing, pickaxe, water bucket and access to the lava shelf. | Seated beside the bed to keep it away from lava; not enclosed. |\n| SC-08 | Believes water controls the visible lava until the lateral source escapes behind stone. | Mines local obsidian; water remains recoverable for rescue. | Seated above, initially calm, then distressed as fire reaches the cabin. |\n| SC-09 | Knows only Moss matters and takes the shortest possible rescue route. | One water source and block-breaking tools; no time to suppress separated fronts. | Dies from fire damage before the protagonist completes the release interaction. No collar or special item drops. |\n| SC-10 | Knows his mining and hidden support created the remembered ruin; grief supplies a new portal objective. | Damaged equipment, locally mined obsidian, remaining spruce and water; safe ignition tools lost. | Dead; never revived, replaced or revealed as secretly safe. |\n| SC-11 | Physically confirms he is the builder and believes immediate activation may return him before the fire. | Exact frame materials, placed lava and spruce ignition cradle; reduced health, no shield and no restored branch torch. | Dead and absent. |\n| SC-12 | Dead after the creeper blast; prior placement continues producing consequences. | Ordinary drops are exposed to lava or fire; the intact frame remains. | Dead. |\n\nThe protagonist may narrate beliefs retrospectively, but statements must remain true to his knowledge at each scene. The narrator may say that he hoped the firebreak would work or that he thought the portal could give another attempt. He may not imply an unseen person, claim the creeper directly created fire, or state a production mechanic as verified before testing.\n\n## Relationship and power balance\n\nThe relationship begins through ordinary taming rather than instant sentiment. Moss follows on timber and food runs, receives food after damage, interferes with building in recognisable wolf fashion and waits beside the bed only when the protagonist descends towards lava. The protagonist releases Moss as soon as he returns.\n\nThe protagonist controls sit, follow and route choice. This is responsible play under normal conditions and the source of vulnerability during the fire. Once Moss is threatened, that vulnerability controls the protagonist’s time and route: he ignores the chest, abandons the structure and moves water solely towards the wolf.\n\nMoss must not be shown in a pen, doghouse, kennel, closed decorative enclosure or separate pet room. The sitting place belongs to the shared cabin interior.\n\n## Locations, geography, travel and blocking\n\n### Fixed layout\n\n- The ravine has a west-rim shelf, a north-wall recessed cave, a narrow stone rib and a recognisable granite seam.\n- The cabin occupies the west shelf because it is the only practical level footprint near the absent portal site.\n- The lower cobblestone foundation bends around the granite seam, producing an L-shaped surviving shell.\n- A protected chest recess is cut into natural stone. It survives visually after the timber upper structure burns.\n- The protagonist’s bed and Moss’s normal sitting position are in the shared main room, one short wall above the stair approach.\n- A player-built stair descends from the cabin towards the north-wall portal cave.\n- A lava shelf lies below the stair. The visible section can be cooled, while a concealed lateral source remains behind the final accessible block.\n- One stripped-spruce support runs continuously behind a later stone-clad interruption and connects the lower route to cabin timber.\n- A lateral cave branch approaches the portal site from beyond the protagonist’s immediate working area. Rescue water removes its torch before the final construction.\n\n### Required repeated compositions\n\n- SC-02 ruined overlook and SC-06 intact overlook use the same foundation, granite and stair alignment.\n- SC-03 opening portal inspection and SC-11 construction use the same cobblestone foot, frame asymmetry, lava lip and plank coordinates.\n- SC-01 and SC-09 use the same water placement, ignored chest, wall break and reach towards Moss; SC-09 continues beyond the earlier cut.\n- SC-12 portal activation resolves into the SC-03 entering-hand composition without depicting two avatars together.\n\n### Travel rules\n\n- Cabin to lava shelf must be long enough for fire to gain time but short enough that the rescue nearly succeeds.\n- The protagonist starts the fire response below the cabin, not beside Moss.\n- The pre-travel protagonist remains far outside the ravine during W0–W4 and arrives only after the older version’s death.\n- No portal transit carries Moss, material or information other than the protagonist’s memory and ordinary inventory.\n\n## Item and material ledger\n\n| Item or material | Origin | Use and final state |\n|---|---|---|\n| Bones | Dropped by an on-screen skeleton during W0/W1 | Used to tame Moss; no looped bones |\n| Spruce | Gathered locally during cabin construction | Cabin, stair support and later ignition cradle; much is consumed by fire |\n| Cobblestone | Mined locally and partly formed by water meeting lava | Foundation, cladding, portal foot and lava-retaining lip |\n| Obsidian | Formed and mined from the W2 lava shelf | Used to build the W4 portal frame; persists to W5 |\n| Water bucket | Part of the protagonist’s expedition kit | Cools lava, contributes to the portal foot, is recovered for rescue and washes away the branch torch |\n| Flint and steel | Ordinary prior equipment stored in or near the cabin | Lost during the fire; absence motivates but does not force the emergency ignition |\n| Spare iron | Ordinary stored supply | Lost with the cabin; mining replacement remains possible but is rejected through haste |\n| Shield and food | Ordinary expedition and homestead supplies | Defence is damaged or lost; health and protection state must be staged credibly before the creeper blast |\n| Portal ignition lava | A local source deliberately placed outside the W4 frame | Released by blast damage towards remaining spruce |\n| Dropped inventory after death | The protagonist’s ordinary carried items | Must be visibly destroyed or credibly absent through tested lava or fire behaviour; no corpse prop |\n\n## Minecraft and world rules\n\n| Rule | Demonstration | Permits | Prevents | Knowledge |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Single self-consistent history | The W5 ruin is created during W3 and the W5 portal during W4 | Effects before causes and one closed personal journey | Branches, resets and replacement outcomes | Audience infers it in SC-06 and confirms it in SC-12 |\n| Personal order differs from world order | W5 entry leads to W0 ejection | The protagonist can build what he previously found | Treating scene order as world chronology | Shown spatially; no scientific speech required |\n| Versions remain geographically separate | Pre-travel self is far away until W5 | One solo avatar on screen throughout | Early self-sighting, silhouette clues and duplicate compositing | Recorded in continuity; not exposition-dependent |\n| The temporal exit needs no standing W0 frame | Portal plane collapses against bare stone | Arrival before physical construction | Materialising an unexplained obsidian exit | Learned by the protagonist in SC-04 |\n| Materials have linear origins | Local mining and gathering source every block | A closed information loop without a closed object loop | Conjured obsidian, wool or invented tokens | Visible to audience through gameplay |\n| Sitting persists until player interaction | Moss waits beside the bed during ravine work | A mechanically grounded rescue constraint | Pen logic, autonomous escape or human reasoning | Established repeatedly in SC-05 |\n| Creeper explosion changes blocks, not fire state | Blast breaks the retaining lip before lava reaches spruce | Explosion-assisted geometry and later ordinary ignition | Claiming a creeper directly creates portal fire | Confirmed visually in SC-12 |\n| Portal activation requires ordinary fire inside a valid frame | Lava ignites surviving spruce and fire forms in the opening | Grounded physical activation around speculative routing | Magical activation caused solely by death or blast | Audience sees the chain; exact geometry needs testing |\n| Proposed Hardcore death is terminal | Final close-range blast ends the protagonist’s personal journey | A closed tragic ending without respawn ambiguity | Casual return after death | Must be approved and clearly established before climax |\n\n## Information continuity\n\n- No other avatar, silhouette, footstep trail, written warning or borrowed voice may imply a second player.\n- Opening evidence consists only of persistent blocks, missing blocks, fluids, fire state, map context and absence.\n- The cold open may withhold Moss’s fate by cutting before the final damage interval. It may not present an alternate rescue or use false narration.\n- Moss never drops a collar, tag or sentimental object. Grief is carried by the empty sitting place and established routine.\n- The protagonist must not know the fire’s hidden route before SC-08 or the creeper’s role before SC-11.\n- The audience must see the hidden support at least once before ignition without having its danger explained.\n- The final activation must show blast, changed retaining geometry, lava access, spruce ignition and portal fire as distinct causal stages.\n\n## Production boundaries and unresolved evidence\n\nThe exact edition, version, difficulty, fire-tick settings and Hardcore presentation are not supplied. Before Story lock, testing must confirm:\n\n- lateral lava flow after the chosen obsidian block is removed;\n- ignition of the concealed support and spread to separated cabin fronts;\n- water collection, direction and torch removal;\n- wolf sitting, damage timing and interaction range;\n- credible player health, armour and shield state before the creeper blast;\n- creeper approach from a mechanically valid dark area;\n- blast effects on the retaining lip without destroying the required frame;\n- lava ignition of remaining spruce and subsequent fire inside the frame;\n- portal persistence until W5;\n- ordinary destruction or absence of dropped items.\n\nThese are candidate mechanics, not validated behaviours. Geometry may change after testing, but any replacement must preserve the same causal responsibilities and environmental pay-offs."}],"questions":[],"warnings":["The Minecraft edition, version, difficulty and fire-tick settings are not supplied; the lava, hidden-support fire, wolf-damage, creeper and portal-ignition chain remains provisional until reproducibly tested.","Hardcore mode, Moss’s irreversible death and the protagonist’s terminal death are proposed structural canon changes and require human approval before Story lock.","The first origin of the portal’s temporal routing is intentionally open in this proposal; no audience, animatic or production validation is claimed."]}
